  {
    "chunk_id": "4014f916-d633-48d0-b59e-a9f5922acdb5",
    "technology": "colbert",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "colbert/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "ColBERT is a fast and accurate retrieval model, enabling scalable BERT-based search over large text collections in tens of milliseconds.\nFigure 1: ColBERT's late interaction, efficiently scoring the fine-grained similarity between a queries and a passage.\nAs Figure 1 illustrates, ColBERT relies on fine-grained contextual late interaction: it encodes each passage into a matrix of token-level embeddings (shown above in blue). Then at search time, it embeds every query into another matrix (shown in green) and efficiently finds passages that contextually match the query using scalable vector-similarity (MaxSim) operators.\nThese rich interactions allow ColBERT to surpass the quality of single-vector representation models, while scaling efficiently to large corpora.\n- ColBERT: Efficient and Effective Passage Search via Contextualized Late Interaction over BERT (SIGIR'20).\n- Relevance-guided Supervision for OpenQA with ColBERT (TACL'21).",
    "character_count": 944
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "98fcd58c-b216-4746-9691-230bed110ced",
    "technology": "colbert",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "colbert/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "- ColBERT: Efficient and Effective Passage Search via Contextualized Late Interaction over BERT (SIGIR'20).\n- Relevance-guided Supervision for OpenQA with ColBERT (TACL'21).\n- Baleen: Robust Multi-Hop Reasoning at Scale via Condensed Retrieval (NeurIPS'21).\n- ColBERTv2: Effective and Efficient Retrieval via Lightweight Late Interaction (NAACL'22).\n- PLAID: An Efficient Engine for Late Interaction Retrieval (CIKM'22).\n- Moving Beyond Downstream Task Accuracy for Information Retrieval Benchmarking (ACL'23 Findings).\n- UDAPDR: Unsupervised Domain Adaptation via LLM Prompting and Distillation of Rerankers (EMNLP'23).\n- (1/28/24) One of the easiest ways to use ColBERT in applications nowadays is the semi-official, fast-growing RAGatouille library.\n- (1/29/23) We have merged a new index updater feature and support for additional Hugging Face models! These are in beta so please give us feedback as you try them out.",
    "character_count": 921
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "ff9ac2f1-f4b2-4745-8ca9-5ba5f9174575",
    "technology": "colbert",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "colbert/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "- (1/29/23) We have merged a new index updater feature and support for additional Hugging Face models! These are in beta so please give us feedback as you try them out.\n- (1/24/23) If you're looking for the DSPy framework for composing retrievers like ColBERTv2 and LLMs, it's at: https://github.com/stanfordnlp/dspy\nThe ColBERTv1 code from the SIGIR'20 paper is in the colbertv1 branch. See here for more information on other branches.\n(Update: nowadays you can typically do pip install colbert-ai[torch,faiss-gpu] to get things up and running, but if you face issues conda is always more reliable for faiss and torch.)\nColBERT requires Python 3.7+ and Pytorch 1.9+ and uses the Hugging Face Transformers library.\nWe strongly recommend creating a conda environment using the commands below. (If you don't have conda, follow the official conda installation guide.)",
    "character_count": 864
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c8a9c4ec-c08c-4825-b4c1-f9d7d7f7f69a",
    "technology": "colbert",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "colbert/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "We strongly recommend creating a conda environment using the commands below. (If you don't have conda, follow the official conda installation guide.)\nWe have also included a new environment file specifically for CPU-only environments (conda_env_cpu.yml), but note that if you are testing CPU execution on a machine that includes GPUs you might need to specify CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"\" as part of your command. Note that a GPU is required for training and indexing.\nconda env create -f conda_env[_cpu].yml\nconda activate colbert\nIf you face any problems, please open a new issue and we'll help you promptly!\nUsing ColBERT on a dataset typically involves the following steps.\nStep 0: Preprocess your collection. At its simplest, ColBERT works with tab-separated (TSV) files: a file (e.g., collection.tsv) will contain all passages and another (e.g., queries.tsv) will contain a set of queries for searching the collection.",
    "character_count": 919
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c81aed81-873a-4b31-ae02-9797cfaf6a2a",
    "technology": "colbert",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "colbert/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "Step 1: Download the pre-trained ColBERTv2 checkpoint. This checkpoint has been trained on the MS MARCO Passage Ranking task. You can also optionally train your own ColBERT model.\nStep 2: Index your collection. Once you have a trained ColBERT model, you need to index your collection to permit fast retrieval. This step encodes all passages into matrices, stores them on disk, and builds data structures for efficient search.\nStep 3: Search the collection with your queries. Given the model and index, you can issue queries over the collection to retrieve the top-k passages for each query.\nBelow, we illustrate these steps via an example run on the MS MARCO Passage Ranking task.\nNEW: We have an experimental notebook on Google Colab that you can use with free GPUs. Indexing 10,000 on the free Colab T4 GPU takes six minutes.\nThis Jupyter notebook docs/intro.ipynb notebook illustrates using the key features of ColBERT with the new Python API.",
    "character_count": 946
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b4d6255d-da8a-470d-bfb5-9f4c255ee404",
    "technology": "colbert",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "colbert/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "This Jupyter notebook docs/intro.ipynb notebook illustrates using the key features of ColBERT with the new Python API.\nIt includes how to download the ColBERTv2 model checkpoint trained on MS MARCO Passage Ranking and how to download our new LoTTE benchmark.\nThis repository works directly with a simple tab-separated file format to store queries, passages, and top-k ranked lists.\n- Queries: each line is qid \\t query text.\n- Collection: each line is pid \\t passage text.\n- Top-k Ranking: each line is qid \\t pid \\t rank.\nThis works directly with the data format of the MS MARCO Passage Ranking dataset. You will need the training triples (triples.train.small.tar.gz), the official top-1000 ranked lists for the dev set queries (top1000.dev), and the dev set relevant passages (qrels.dev.small.tsv). For indexing the full collection, you will also need the list of passages (collection.tar.gz).\nFor fast retrieval, indexing precomputes the ColBERT representations of passages.\nExample usage:",
