  {
    "chunk_id": "a1cd137c-73e6-4c6d-95e0-61c3139c9147",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "This repository releases the source code for the paper:\n- Corrective Retrieval Augmented Generation.\nShi-Qi Yan, Jia-Chen Gu, Yun Zhu, Zhen-Hua Ling",
    "character_count": 148
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "418e4d21-f9b7-4422-896a-8644e998a0a9",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "Large language models (LLMs) inevitably exhibit hallucinations since the accuracy of generated texts cannot be secured solely by the parametric knowledge they encapsulate. Although retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is a practicable complement to LLMs, it relies heavily on the relevance of retrieved documents, raising concerns about how the model behaves if retrieval goes wrong. To this end, we propose the Corrective Retrieval Augmented Generation (CRAG) to improve the robustness of generation. Specifically, a lightweight retrieval evaluator is designed to assess the overall quality of retrieved documents for a query, returning a confidence degree based on which different knowledge retrieval actions can be triggered. Since retrieval from static and limited corpora can only return sub-optimal documents, large-scale web searches are utilized as an extension for augmenting the retrieval results",
    "character_count": 906
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "5148280d-2c9c-42ce-81c1-9813ece8fb65",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": ". Since retrieval from static and limited corpora can only return sub-optimal documents, large-scale web searches are utilized as an extension for augmenting the retrieval results. Besides, a decompose-then-recompose algorithm is designed for retrieved documents to selectively focus on key information and filter out irrelevant information in them. CRAG is plug-and-play and can be seamlessly coupled with various RAG-based approaches. Experiments on four datasets covering short- and long-form generation tasks show that CRAG can significantly improve the performance of RAG-based approaches.",
    "character_count": 594
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c4b397e6-da22-45ed-a1f7-9e075ac34bdc",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "- 2024-10-08: Revise the prompt format in the inference stage to improve the performance in RAG and CRAG modes and make the generator follow instructions better.\n- 2024-03-04: Release the knowledge preparation including knowledge refinement and knowledge search to gather correct, incorrect and ambiguous knowledge.\n- 2024-03-02: Release the fine-tuning of the evaluator and training data preprocessed on PopQA.\n- 2024-03-01: Release the data preprocess and data preparation for the inference of Self-CRAG.\n- 2024-02-22: Release the inference of CRAG and the weights of the retrieval evaluator used in our experiments. Will release the inference of Self-CRAG and the fine-tuning of the retrieval evaluator soon.\nNote: We use Python 3.11 for CRAG To get started, install conda and run:\ngit clone https://github.com/HuskyInSalt/CRAG.git\nconda create -n CRAG python=3.11\n...\npip install -r requirements.txt",
    "character_count": 903
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "ec0751b7-d94d-49bc-a0e1-ffd9263094e3",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "Note: We use Python 3.11 for CRAG To get started, install conda and run:\ngit clone https://github.com/HuskyInSalt/CRAG.git\nconda create -n CRAG python=3.11\n...\npip install -r requirements.txt\n- Download the eval_data created by Self-RAG (Asai et al., 2023) on PopQA, PubQA, Bio and Arc_challenge with retrieved results\n- Download the LLaMA-2 fine-tuned by Self-RAG (Asai et al., 2023).\n- Download the fine-tuned weights of the retrieval evaluator used in our experiments.\n- Download the training data for the evaluator.\nRun the following command to preprocess the dataset for questions and retrieval results. Specifically for PopQA, the label of each (question, passage) pair is also collected.\nbash run_data_preprocess.sh\nRun the following command to fine-tune the evaluator.\nbash run_evaluator_training.sh\nThe training data is shared and can be downloaded, the method of label collection is similar to the test set preparation in scripts/data_process.py.",
    "character_count": 956
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fab1dc57-b73a-4f75-8e20-9b8d5c99a721",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "bash run_evaluator_training.sh\nThe training data is shared and can be downloaded, the method of label collection is similar to the test set preparation in scripts/data_process.py.\nRun the following command to gather knowledge for inference, including correct, incorrect and ambiguous.\nbash run_knowledge_preparation.sh\nSpecifically, you can also run the following commands individually.\nAccording to the paper, we decompose the retrieval results and filter out irrelevant parts. Three modes are listed to decompose passages: fixed_num, excerption and selection.\nfixed_num segments passages into a fixed number of words, 'excerption' segments passages based on the end of the sentences, while passages are not divided in selection mode.\nYou can choose the mode by --decompose_mode.\npython internal_knowledge_preparation.py \\\n--model_path YOUR_EVALUATOR_PATH \\\n--input_queries ../data/$dataset/sources \\\n--input_retrieval ../data/$dataset/retrieved_psgs \\\n--decompose_mode selection \\",
    "character_count": 982
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6b4b4479-7074-43db-b426-767e951dcd23",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "--model_path YOUR_EVALUATOR_PATH \\\n--input_queries ../data/$dataset/sources \\\n--input_retrieval ../data/$dataset/retrieved_psgs \\\n--decompose_mode selection \\\n--output_file ../data/$dataset/ref/correct\nQuestion rewriting and web searching are proposed here, thus an openai_api_key and a search_key are required.\nIn this experiment, we utilized a third-party Google Search API platform for searching.\nTwo selective modes including wiki and all are available.\nwiki visits pages related to Wikipedia preferentially, while all visit all pages equally.\npython external_knowledge_preparation.py \\\n--model_path YOUR_EVALUATOR_PATH \\\n--input_queries ../data/$dataset/sources \\\n--openai_key $OPENAI_KEY \\\n--search_key $SEARCH_KEY \\\n--task $dataset --mode wiki\\\n--output_file ../data/$dataset/ref/incorrect\nRun the following command to combine both correct and incorrect knowledge for ambiguous action.\npython combined_knowledge_preparation.py \\\n--correct_path ../data/$dataset/ref/correct \\",
    "character_count": 981
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "2a54c2e6-d8bb-4641-844f-5abe53cf2840",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "Run the following command to combine both correct and incorrect knowledge for ambiguous action.\npython combined_knowledge_preparation.py \\\n--correct_path ../data/$dataset/ref/correct \\\n--incorrect_path ../data/$dataset/ref/incorrect \\\n--ambiguous_path ../data/$dataset/ref/ambiguous\nRun the following command for CRAG inference.\nbash run_crag_inference.sh\nRun the following command for Self-CRAG data preparation.\nbash run_selfcrag_preparation.sh\nWith this command, the retrieval results of the original input files of Self-RAG will be replaced by correct, incorrect and ambiguous context. Then follow the instructions at Self-RAG (Asai et al., 2023) for the ultimate results.\nFor Bio evaluation, please follow the instructions at the FactScore (Min et al., 2023) official repository.\npython -m factscore.factscorer --data_path YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE --model_name retrieval+ChatGPT --cache_dir YOUR_CACHE_DIR --openai_key YOUR_OPEN_AI_KEY --verbose",
    "character_count": 942
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f0a9346c-8c29-45ca-baa8-1c8fa05f7ee4",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "python -m factscore.factscorer --data_path YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE --model_name retrieval+ChatGPT --cache_dir YOUR_CACHE_DIR --openai_key YOUR_OPEN_AI_KEY --verbose\nIt is worth mentioning that, previous FactScore adopted text-davinci-003 by default, which has been deprecated since 2024-01-04 and replaced by gpt-3.5-turbo-instruct. Both results of CRAG and Self-CRAG reported are based on the text-davinci-003, which may differ from the current gpt-3.5-turbo-instruct evaluation.\nFor the other datasets, run the following command.\nbash run_eval.sh\ne.g., PopQA\npython eval.py \\\n--input_file eval_data/popqa_longtail_w_gs.jsonl \\\n--eval_file ../data/popqa/output/YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE \\\n--metric match\nPubHealth\npython eval.py \\\n--input_file eval_data/health_claims_processed.jsonl \\\n--eval_file ../data/pubqa/output/YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE \\\n--metric match --task fever\nArc_Challenge\npython run_test_eval.py \\\n--input_file eval_data/arc_challenge_processed.jsonl \\",
    "character_count": 945
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "3c86c371-7864-41b9-aaf9-c1f58c4a920b",
    "technology": "crag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "crag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "text": "--eval_file ../data/pubqa/output/YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE \\\n--metric match --task fever\nArc_Challenge\npython run_test_eval.py \\\n--input_file eval_data/arc_challenge_processed.jsonl \\\n--eval_file ../data/arc_challenge/output/YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE \\\n--metric match --task arc_c\nIf you think our work is helpful or use the code, please cite the following paper:\n@article{yan2024corrective,\ntitle={Corrective Retrieval Augmented Generation},\nauthor={Yan, Shi-Qi and Gu, Jia-Chen and Zhu, Yun and Ling, Zhen-Hua},\njournal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2401.15884},\nyear={2024}\n}",
    "character_count": 552
  },
  {
