    "path": "google_cloud_run/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "Data processing: Lightweight data transformation\nBuild Cloud Run data processing applications that transform lightweight data as it arrives and store it as structured data. Transformations can be triggered from Google Cloud sources. When a .csv file is created, an event is fired and delivered to a Cloud Run service. Data is then extracted, structured, and stored in a BigQuery table.\nAutomation: Scheduled document generation\nSchedule a monthly job with Cloud Scheduler to generate invoices using a Cloud Run service. Because containers containing custom binaries can be deployed to Cloud Run, it is able to run in a PDF generation tool like LibreOffice in a serverless way, which means only paying when you are generating invoices.\nAutomation: Business workflow with webhooks",
    "character_count": 778
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "0b4269b4-ed7b-4d65-a34a-daf9c3d07374",
    "technology": "google_cloud_run",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "google_cloud_run/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "Automation: Business workflow with webhooks\nConnect your operations together with an event‐driven approach. Cloud Run scales on demand while implementing a webhook target, pushing events in the form of requests and only charging you when you receive and process the event. React to events from GitHub or Slack, or send webhooks when a purchase is made, a job is ready, or an alert is fired with a service that can react on a just‐in‐time basis to trigger a microservice in your infrastructure.\nMigrating Node.js apps from Heroku to Cloud Run\nLearn how to migrate Node.js web apps that are running on Heroku to Cloud Run on Google Cloud. This tutorial is intended for architects and product owners who want to migrate their apps from Heroku to managed services on Google Cloud.\nModernization path for .NET applications on Google Cloud",
    "character_count": 833
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6cda9ea0-186a-4e9d-b072-42d35bebf641",
    "technology": "google_cloud_run",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "google_cloud_run/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "Modernization path for .NET applications on Google Cloud\nThis document looks at the common limitations of monolithic applications and describes a gradual yet structured process for modernizing them. This document is intended for cloud architects, system administrators, and CTOs who are familiar with Windows and the .NET ecosystem and want to learn more about what modernization involves.\nStarting a Cloud Run project from a template\nStart your app from a template within Intellij, including Flask, Django, Node.js, Java, and Go templates.\nNode.js samples\nIncludes HelloWorld, Pub/Sub, Cloud SQL examples, image processing, and many others.\nGo samples\nIncludes HelloWorld, Pub/Sub, Cloud SQL examples, image processing, and many others.\nJava samples\nIncludes HelloWorld, Pub/Sub, Cloud SQL examples, image processing, and many others.",
    "character_count": 835
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "baf247fc-72b1-481a-84b9-2fbc79c52d5d",
