  {
    "chunk_id": "6ebfc53a-ff3f-4bc1-a9b1-759e7b398fe7",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Lightweight Kubernetes. Production ready, easy to install, half the memory, all in a binary less than 100 MB.\nGreat for:\n- Edge\n- IoT\n- CI\n- Development\n- ARM\n- Embedding k8s\n- Situations where a PhD in k8s clusterology is infeasible\nK3s is a fully conformant production-ready Kubernetes distribution with the following changes:\n- It is packaged as a single binary.\n- It adds support for sqlite3 as the default storage backend. Etcd3, MariaDB, MySQL, and Postgres are also supported.\n- It wraps Kubernetes and other components in a single, simple launcher.\n- It is secure by default with reasonable defaults for lightweight environments.\n- It has minimal to no OS dependencies (just a sane kernel and cgroup mounts needed).\n- It eliminates the need to expose a port on Kubernetes worker nodes for the kubelet API by exposing this API to the Kubernetes control plane nodes over a websocket tunnel.\nK3s bundles the following technologies together into a single cohesive distribution:",
    "character_count": 981
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "7bf76501-188e-4e54-959e-bb33d4d413d5",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "K3s bundles the following technologies together into a single cohesive distribution:\n- Containerd & runc\n- Flannel for CNI\n- CoreDNS\n- Metrics Server\n- Traefik for ingress\n- Klipper-lb as an embedded service load balancer provider\n- Kube-router netpol controller for network policy\n- Helm-controller to allow for CRD-driven deployment of helm manifests\n- Kine as a datastore shim that allows etcd to be replaced with other databases\n- Local-path-provisioner for provisioning volumes using local storage\n- Host utilities such as iptables/nftables, ebtables, ethtool, & socat\nThese technologies can be disabled or swapped out for technologies of your choice.\nAdditionally, K3s simplifies Kubernetes operations by maintaining functionality for:\n- Managing the TLS certificates of Kubernetes components\n- Managing the connection between worker and server nodes\n- Auto-deploying Kubernetes resources from local manifests in realtime as they are changed.\n- Managing an embedded etcd cluster",
    "character_count": 984
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "588f08c2-f7c3-4cd1-a116-80269de9798c",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "- Managing the connection between worker and server nodes\n- Auto-deploying Kubernetes resources from local manifests in realtime as they are changed.\n- Managing an embedded etcd cluster\nWe wanted an installation of Kubernetes that was half the size in terms of memory footprint. Kubernetes is a 10 letter word stylized as k8s. So something half as big as Kubernetes would be a 5 letter word stylized as K3s. A '3' is also an '8' cut in half vertically. There is neither a long-form of K3s nor official pronunciation.",
    "character_count": 516
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "28ad53fb-8e13-44d1-a97b-ef56cc18a19b",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "No, it's a distribution. A fork implies continued divergence from the original. This is not K3s's goal or practice. K3s explicitly intends not to change any core Kubernetes functionality. We seek to remain as close to upstream Kubernetes as possible. However, we maintain a small set of patches (well under 1000 lines) important to K3s's use case and deployment model. We maintain patches for other components as well. When possible, we contribute these changes back to the upstream projects, for example, with SELinux support in containerd. This is a common practice amongst software distributions.",
    "character_count": 599
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "7cafb7f5-be9a-4d65-b650-95ed195f4e44",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "K3s is a distribution because it packages additional components and services necessary for a fully functional cluster that go beyond vanilla Kubernetes. These are opinionated choices on technologies for components like ingress, storage class, network policy, service load balancer, and even container runtime. These choices and technologies are touched on in more detail in the What is this? section.\nThere are two major ways that K3s is lighter weight than upstream Kubernetes:\n- The memory footprint to run it is smaller\n- The binary, which contains all the non-containerized components needed to run a cluster, is smaller\nThe memory footprint is reduced primarily by running many components inside of a single process. This eliminates significant overhead that would otherwise be duplicated for each component.\nThe binary is smaller by removing third-party storage drivers and cloud providers, explained in more detail below.",
    "character_count": 928
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "ef86f1e0-f3db-4aed-a425-f0f10b30bf7c",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "The binary is smaller by removing third-party storage drivers and cloud providers, explained in more detail below.\nThis is a common point of confusion because it has changed over time. Early versions of K3s had much more removed than the current version. K3s currently removes two things:\n- In-tree storage drivers\n- In-tree cloud provider\nBoth of these have out-of-tree alternatives in the form of CSI and CCM, which work in K3s and which upstream is moving towards.\nWe remove these to achieve a smaller binary size. They can be removed while remaining conformant because neither affects core Kubernetes functionality. They are also dependent on third-party cloud or data center technologies/services, which may not be available in many K3s' use cases.\nJoin Slack to chat with K3s developers and other K3s users. Great place to learn and ask questions: #k3s and #k3s-contributor and #k3s channel in CNCF Slack\nGitHub Issues - Submit your issues and feature requests via GitHub.",
    "character_count": 978
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9517d9ef-e96a-4489-a6f3-fb8982c22831",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "GitHub Issues - Submit your issues and feature requests via GitHub.\nThe K3s developer community hangs out on Zoom to chat. Everybody is welcome.\nAdd the Linux Foundation iCal to your calendar:\n- AMS/EMEA TZ 10:00 am PST - every second Tuesday of the month\n- EMEA/APAC TimeZone friendly - every third Tuesday of the month\nMeeting notes and agenda: https://hackmd.io/@k3s/meet-notes/\nMeeting recordings: K3s Channel\nYou can check also the full details on the website: https://k3s.io/community\nCheck out our roadmap to see what we have planned moving forward.\nK3s maintains pace with upstream Kubernetes releases. Our goal is to release patch releases within one week, and new minors within 30 days.",
    "character_count": 696
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "155ae5c8-7f41-4005-9154-c8c28151616b",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "K3s maintains pace with upstream Kubernetes releases. Our goal is to release patch releases within one week, and new minors within 30 days.\nOur release versioning reflects the version of upstream Kubernetes that is being released. For example, the K3s release v1.27.4+k3s1 maps to the v1.27.4 Kubernetes release. We add a postfix in the form of +k3s<number> to allow us to make additional releases using the same version of upstream Kubernetes while remaining semver compliant. For example, if we discovered a high severity bug in v1.27.4+k3s1 and needed to release an immediate fix for it, we would release v1.27.4+k3s2.\nPlease see the official docs site for complete documentation.\nThe install.sh script provides a convenient way to download K3s and add a service to systemd or openrc.\nTo install k3s as a service, run:\ncurl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -A kubeconfig file is written to /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml and the service is automatically started or restarted.",
    "character_count": 969
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "408a2fc1-a72b-4c96-9057-4c9fa5a7a4da",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "To install k3s as a service, run:\ncurl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -A kubeconfig file is written to /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml and the service is automatically started or restarted.\nThe install script will install K3s and additional utilities, such as kubectl, crictl, k3s-killall.sh, and k3s-uninstall.sh, for example:\nsudo kubectl get nodesK3S_TOKEN is created at /var/lib/rancher/k3s/server/node-token on your server.\nTo install on worker nodes, pass K3S_URL along with\nK3S_TOKEN environment variables, for example:\ncurl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | K3S_URL=https://myserver:6443 K3S_TOKEN=XXX sh -- Download k3sfrom latest release, x86_64, armhf, arm64 and s390x are supported.\n- Run the server.\nsudo k3s server &\n# Kubeconfig is written to /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml\nsudo k3s kubectl get nodes\n# On a different node run the below. NODE_TOKEN comes from\n# /var/lib/rancher/k3s/server/node-token on your server",
    "character_count": 909
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6b9e008f-7016-4385-8046-1eda9e95c23d",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "k3s/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "text": "# Kubeconfig is written to /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml\nsudo k3s kubectl get nodes\n# On a different node run the below. NODE_TOKEN comes from\n# /var/lib/rancher/k3s/server/node-token on your server\nsudo k3s agent --server https://myserver:6443 --token ${NODE_TOKEN}Please check out our contributing guide if you're interested in contributing to K3s.\nSecurity issues in K3s can be reported by sending an email to security@k3s.io. Please do not file issues about security issues.",
    "character_count": 474
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6b021128-d849-4370-b1de-22f2ea51a09d",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "k3s/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Lightweight Kubernetes. Easy to install, half the memory, all in a binary of less than 100 MB.\nGreat for:\n- Edge\n- Homelab\n- Internet of Things (IoT)\n- Continuous Integration (CI)\n- Development\n- Single board computers (ARM)\n- Air-gapped environments\n- Embedded K8s\n- Situations where a PhD in K8s clusterology is infeasible\nWhat is K3s?\nK3s is a fully compliant Kubernetes distribution with the following enhancements:\n- Distributed as a single binary or minimal container image.\n- Lightweight datastore based on sqlite3 as the default storage backend. etcd3, MySQL, and Postgres are also available.\n- Wrapped in simple launcher that handles a lot of the complexity of TLS and options.\n- Secure by default with reasonable defaults for lightweight environments.\n- Operation of all Kubernetes control plane components is encapsulated in a single binary and process, allowing K3s to automate and manage complex cluster operations like distributing certificates.",
    "character_count": 959
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "8208878e-4bd3-49f9-b7d1-9b9acac18cc4",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "k3s/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "- Operation of all Kubernetes control plane components is encapsulated in a single binary and process, allowing K3s to automate and manage complex cluster operations like distributing certificates.\n- External dependencies have been minimized; the only requirements are a modern kernel and cgroup mounts.\n- Packages the required dependencies for easy \"batteries-included\" cluster creation:\n- containerd / cri-dockerd container runtime (CRI)\n- Flannel Container Network Interface (CNI)\n- CoreDNS Cluster DNS\n- Traefik Ingress controller\n- ServiceLB Load-Balancer controller\n- Kube-router Network Policy controller\n- Local-path-provisioner Persistent Volume controller\n- Spegel distributed container image registry mirror\n- Host utilities (iptables, socat, etc)",
    "character_count": 758
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e7f41214-fba6-4a84-b307-25a546b80d35",
    "technology": "k3s",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "k3s/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "What's with the name?\nWe wanted an installation of Kubernetes that was half the size in terms of memory footprint. Kubernetes is a 10-letter word stylized as K8s. So something half as big as Kubernetes would be a 5-letter word stylized as K3s. There is no long form of K3s and no official pronunciation.",
    "character_count": 303
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "ee429af2-2585-4dee-9f3e-805fe18daf92",
    "technology": "kserve",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "kserve/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "KServe is a standardized distributed generative and predictive AI inference platform for scalable, multi-framework deployment on Kubernetes.\nKServe is being used by many organizations and is a Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF) incubating project.\nFor more details, visit the KServe website.\nSingle platform that unifies Generative and Predictive AI inference on Kubernetes. Simple enough for quick deployments, yet powerful enough to handle enterprise-scale AI workloads with advanced features.\nGenerative AI\n- 🧮 Optimized Backends: Support for vLLM and llm-d for optimized performance for serving LLMs\n- 📌 Standardization: OpenAI-compatible inference protocol for seamless integration with LLMs\n- 🚅 GPU Acceleration: High-performance serving with GPU support and optimized memory management for large models\n- 💾 Model Caching: Intelligent model caching to reduce loading times and improve response latency for frequently used models",
    "character_count": 940
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "3871e820-295f-42b7-a9c7-435e9f6bf5a1",
    "technology": "kserve",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "kserve/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "- 💾 Model Caching: Intelligent model caching to reduce loading times and improve response latency for frequently used models\n- 🗂️ KV Cache Offloading: Advanced memory management with KV cache offloading to CPU/disk for handling longer sequences efficiently\n- 📈 Autoscaling: Request-based autoscaling capabilities optimized for generative workload patterns\n- 🔧 Hugging Face Ready: Native support for Hugging Face models with streamlined deployment workflows\nPredictive AI\n- 🧮 Multi-Framework: Support for TensorFlow, PyTorch, scikit-learn, XGBoost, ONNX, and more\n- 🔀 Intelligent Routing: Seamless request routing between predictor, transformer, and explainer components with automatic traffic management\n- 🔄 Advanced Deployments: Canary rollouts, inference pipelines, and ensembles with InferenceGraph\n- ⚡ Autoscaling: Request-based autoscaling with scale-to-zero for predictive workloads",
    "character_count": 888
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9a6ecded-d863-42c0-8f04-a2dc43a5c2e6",
    "technology": "kserve",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "kserve/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "- 🔄 Advanced Deployments: Canary rollouts, inference pipelines, and ensembles with InferenceGraph\n- ⚡ Autoscaling: Request-based autoscaling with scale-to-zero for predictive workloads\n- 🔍 Model Explainability: Built-in support for model explanations and feature attribution to understand prediction reasoning\n- 📊 Advanced Monitoring: Enables payload logging, outlier detection, adversarial detection, and drift detection\n- 💰 Cost Efficient: Scale-to-zero on expensive resources when not in use, reducing infrastructure costs\nTo learn more about KServe, how to use various supported features, and how to participate in the KServe community, please follow the KServe website documentation. Additionally, we have compiled a list of presentations and demos to dive through various details.",
    "character_count": 786
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "db7cf522-f21e-4594-9b00-bd90b423c72a",
    "technology": "kserve",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "kserve/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "- Standard Kubernetes Installation: Compared to Serverless Installation, this is a more lightweight installation. However, this option does not support canary deployment and request based autoscaling with scale-to-zero.\n- Knative Installation: KServe by default installs Knative for serverless deployment for InferenceService.\n- ModelMesh Installation: You can optionally install ModelMesh to enable high-scale, high-density and frequently-changing model serving use cases.\n- Quick Installation: Install KServe on your local machine.\nKServe is an important addon component of Kubeflow, please learn more from the Kubeflow KServe documentation. Check out the following guides for running on AWS or on OpenShift Container Platform.\n💡 Roadmap\n🤝 Adopters\nThanks to all of our amazing contributors!",
    "character_count": 793
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "1aa6842a-2f7d-4122-a1fb-0e79b7f842c4",
    "technology": "kubernetes",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "kubernetes/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Kubernetes, also known as K8s, is an open source system for managing containerized applications across multiple hosts. It provides basic mechanisms for the deployment, maintenance, and scaling of applications.\nKubernetes builds upon a decade and a half of experience at Google running production workloads at scale using a system called Borg, combined with best-of-breed ideas and practices from the community.\nKubernetes is hosted by the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF). If your company wants to help shape the evolution of technologies that are container-packaged, dynamically scheduled, and microservices-oriented, consider joining the CNCF. For details about who's involved and how Kubernetes plays a role, read the CNCF announcement.\nSee our documentation on kubernetes.io.\nTake a free course on Scalable Microservices with Kubernetes.\nTo use Kubernetes code as a library in other applications, see the list of published components.",
    "character_count": 946
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "194882eb-463a-4c09-a70d-dfba153b1cb4",
    "technology": "kubernetes",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "kubernetes/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "See our documentation on kubernetes.io.\nTake a free course on Scalable Microservices with Kubernetes.\nTo use Kubernetes code as a library in other applications, see the list of published components.\nUse of the k8s.io/kubernetes module or k8s.io/kubernetes/... packages as libraries is not supported.\nThe community repository hosts all information about building Kubernetes from source, how to contribute code and documentation, who to contact about what, etc.\nIf you want to build Kubernetes right away there are two options:\nYou have a working Go environment.\ngit clone https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes\ncd kubernetes\nmake\nYou have a working Docker environment.\ngit clone https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes\ncd kubernetes\nmake quick-release\nFor the full story, head over to the developer's documentation.\nIf you need support, start with the troubleshooting guide, and work your way through the process that we've outlined.",
    "character_count": 935
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "2f199302-bc3a-4906-939e-3e7d4d2a7b84",
    "technology": "kubernetes",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "kubernetes/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "For the full story, head over to the developer's documentation.\nIf you need support, start with the troubleshooting guide, and work your way through the process that we've outlined.\nThat said, if you have questions, reach out to us one way or another.\nThe Calendar has the list of all the meetings in the Kubernetes community in a single location.\nThe User Case Studies website has real-world use cases of organizations across industries that are deploying/migrating to Kubernetes.\nKubernetes project is governed by a framework of principles, values, policies and processes to help our community and constituents towards our shared goals.\nThe Kubernetes Community is the launching point for learning about how we organize ourselves.\nThe Kubernetes Steering community repo is used by the Kubernetes Steering Committee, which oversees governance of the Kubernetes project.\nThe Kubernetes Enhancements repo provides information about Kubernetes releases, as well as feature tracking and backlogs.",
    "character_count": 993
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "128c55b8-6efb-4bb8-9f49-cbd74aed9bf6",
    "technology": "kubernetes",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "kubernetes/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Kubernetes is an open source container orchestration engine for automating deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications. The open source project is hosted by the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF).\nLearn about Kubernetes and its fundamental concepts.\nFollow tutorials to learn how to deploy applications in Kubernetes.\nGet Kubernetes running based on your resources and needs.\nLook up common tasks and how to perform them using a short sequence of steps.\nBrowse terminology, command line syntax, API resource types, and setup tool documentation.\nFind out how you can help make Kubernetes better.\nGet certified in Kubernetes and make your cloud native projects successful!\nInstall Kubernetes or upgrade to the newest version.\nThis website contains documentation for the current and previous 4 versions of Kubernetes.",
    "character_count": 842
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e8517fc4-b422-4102-8f18-2408c19e714f",
    "technology": "langchain",
    "source": "api_reference",
    "path": "langchain/api_reference.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Reference Docs\nUnified API reference documentation for LangChain, LangGraph, Deep Agents, LangSmith, and integrations. Browse Python and TypeScript packages, explore classes, functions, and types across the entire LangChain ecosystem.\nLooking for guides, tutorials, and conceptual docs? Visit the main documentation site.",
    "character_count": 321
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b4982804-4922-41d4-9d9e-12270d087d04",
    "technology": "langchain",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "langchain/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "LangChain is a framework for building agents and LLM-powered applications. It helps you chain together interoperable components and third-party integrations to simplify AI application development — all while future-proofing decisions as the underlying technology evolves.\nTip\nJust getting started? Check out Deep Agents — a higher-level package built on LangChain for agents that have built-in capabilites for common usage patterns such as planning, subagents, file system usage, and more.\nuv add langchainfrom langchain.chat_models import init_chat_model\nmodel = init_chat_model(\"openai:gpt-5.5\")\nresult = model.invoke(\"Hello, world!\")If you're looking for more advanced customization or agent orchestration, check out LangGraph, our framework for building controllable agent workflows.\nFor an equivalent JS/TS library, check out LangChain.js.\nTip\nFor developing, debugging, and deploying AI agents and LLM applications, see LangSmith.",
    "character_count": 936
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "bbf9c582-7886-408a-b97f-ee180e53fa3d",
    "technology": "langchain",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "langchain/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "For an equivalent JS/TS library, check out LangChain.js.\nTip\nFor developing, debugging, and deploying AI agents and LLM applications, see LangSmith.\nWhile the LangChain framework can be used standalone, it also integrates seamlessly with any LangChain product, giving developers a full suite of tools when building LLM applications.\n- Deep Agents — Build agents that can plan, use subagents, and leverage file systems for complex tasks\n- LangGraph — Build agents that can reliably handle complex tasks with our low-level agent orchestration framework\n- Integrations — Chat & embedding models, tools & toolkits, and more\n- LangSmith — Agent evals, observability, and debugging for LLM apps\n- LangSmith Deployment — Deploy and scale agents with a purpose-built platform for long-running, stateful workflows\nLangChain helps developers build applications powered by LLMs through a standard interface for models, embeddings, vector stores, and more.",
    "character_count": 944
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9bfea12f-0d9d-45c0-827b-d7d109b289f0",
    "technology": "langchain",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "langchain/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "LangChain helps developers build applications powered by LLMs through a standard interface for models, embeddings, vector stores, and more.\n- Real-time data augmentation — Easily connect LLMs to diverse data sources and external/internal systems, drawing from LangChain's vast library of integrations with model providers, tools, vector stores, retrievers, and more\n- Model interoperability — Swap models in and out as your engineering team experiments to find the best choice for your application's needs. As the industry frontier evolves, adapt quickly — LangChain's abstractions keep you moving without losing momentum\n- Rapid prototyping — Quickly build and iterate on LLM applications with LangChain's modular, component-based architecture. Test different approaches and workflows without rebuilding from scratch, accelerating your development cycle",
    "character_count": 854
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "318be744-d0b4-4797-9d3f-e590c33a7c76",
    "technology": "langchain",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "langchain/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "- Production-ready features — Deploy reliable applications with built-in support for monitoring, evaluation, and debugging through integrations like LangSmith. Scale with confidence using battle-tested patterns and best practices\n- Vibrant community and ecosystem — Leverage a rich ecosystem of integrations, templates, and community-contributed components. Benefit from continuous improvements and stay up-to-date with the latest AI developments through an active open-source community\n- Flexible abstraction layers — Work at the level of abstraction that suits your needs — from high-level chains for quick starts to low-level components for fine-grained control. LangChain grows with your application's complexity\n- Documentation — conceptual overviews and guides\n- LangChain ecosystem overview — how LangChain, LangGraph, and Deep Agents fit together\n- API reference — complete reference for all public classes, functions, and types",
    "character_count": 936
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "bc12e096-863c-4895-ae1d-5f20b04f94ad",
    "technology": "langchain",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "langchain/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "- LangChain ecosystem overview — how LangChain, LangGraph, and Deep Agents fit together\n- API reference — complete reference for all public classes, functions, and types\n- Discussions — community forum for technical questions, ideas, and feedback\n- LangChain Academy — comprehensive, free courses on LangChain libraries and products, made by the LangChain team\n- Contributing Guide — how to contribute and find good first issues\n- Code of Conduct — community guidelines and standards",
    "character_count": 483
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "4d1ac344-b59f-45b3-95ed-6175794b4463",
    "technology": "langchain",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "langchain/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "create_agent: a minimal, highly configurable harness. The harness is everything around the model loop: the prompt, the tools, and any middleware that shapes behavior. Start with the primitives and compose exactly what your use case needs. Supports OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and more.\nCreate an agent\nThis example demonstrates how to create a simple LangChain agent with a custom tool:Core benefits\nStandard model interface\nUse one interface for chat models, embeddings, and more across providers. Switch models with minimal code changes and keep your application portable as requirements evolve.\nHighly configurable harness\nStart with\ncreate_agent as a minimal harness and add capabilities incrementally through middleware. Compose only what your use case needs, from guardrails and retries to routing and custom tool policies.Built on top of LangGraph",
    "character_count": 853
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "92a34dc1-1132-4a75-9c1c-b9ae2995d0af",
    "technology": "langchain",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "langchain/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "LangChain’s agents are built on top of LangGraph. This allows us to take advantage of LangGraph’s durable execution, human-in-the-loop support, persistence, and more.\nDebug with LangSmith\nInspect traces, tool calls, state transitions, and latency in one place. Find failure modes, evaluate quality, and improve agent behavior with execution data.\nConnect these docs to Claude, VSCode, and more via MCP for real-time answers.",
    "character_count": 424
  },
