    "chunk_id": "c5715136-54b2-473b-8442-7dd228534868",
    "technology": "mlserver",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "mlserver/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "An open source inference server for your machine learning models.\nMLServer aims to provide an easy way to start serving your machine learning models through a REST and gRPC interface, fully compliant with KFServing's V2 Dataplane spec. Watch a quick video introducing the project here.\n- Multi-model serving, letting users run multiple models within the same process.\n- Ability to run inference in parallel for vertical scaling across multiple models through a pool of inference workers.\n- Support for adaptive batching, to group inference requests together on the fly.\n- Scalability with deployment in Kubernetes native frameworks, including Seldon Core and KServe (formerly known as KFServing), where MLServer is the core Python inference server used to serve machine learning models.\n- Support for the standard V2 Inference Protocol on both the gRPC and REST flavours, which has been standardised and adopted by various model serving frameworks.",
    "character_count": 948
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e5925666-9739-4c3b-a4f6-a69ca5b002f0",
    "technology": "mlserver",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "mlserver/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "- Support for the standard V2 Inference Protocol on both the gRPC and REST flavours, which has been standardised and adopted by various model serving frameworks.\nYou can read more about the goals of this project on the initial design document.\nYou can install the mlserver package running:\npip install mlserverNote that to use any of the optional inference runtimes,\nyou'll need to install the relevant package.\nFor example, to serve a scikit-learn model, you would need to install the\nmlserver-sklearn package:\npip install mlserver-sklearnFor further information on how to use MLServer, you can check any of the available examples.\nInference runtimes allow you to define how your model should be used within MLServer. You can think of them as the backend glue between MLServer and your machine learning framework of choice. You can read more about inference runtimes in their documentation page.",
    "character_count": 896
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b08dcd65-4dec-42c5-90be-48bd3b7c5859",
    "technology": "mlserver",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "mlserver/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "Out of the box, MLServer comes with a set of pre-packaged runtimes which let you interact with a subset of common frameworks. This allows you to start serving models saved in these frameworks straight away. However, it's also possible to write custom runtimes.\nOut of the box, MLServer provides support for:\n| Framework | Supported | Documentation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scikit-Learn | ✅ | MLServer SKLearn |\n| XGBoost | ✅ | MLServer XGBoost |\n| Spark MLlib | ✅ | MLServer MLlib |\n| LightGBM | ✅ | MLServer LightGBM |\n| CatBoost | ✅ | MLServer CatBoost |\n| Tempo | ✅ | github.com/SeldonIO/tempo |\n| MLflow | ✅ | MLServer MLflow |\n| Alibi-Detect | ✅ | MLServer Alibi Detect |\n| Alibi-Explain | ✅ | MLServer Alibi Explain |\n| HuggingFace | ✅ | MLServer HuggingFace |",
    "character_count": 759
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "08e735d8-c3b9-424d-8669-72316a80054a",
