    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "# Step 3: Sending an Inference Request\n# In a separate console, launch the image_client example from the NGC Triton SDK container\ndocker run -it --rm --net=host nvcr.io/nvidia/tritonserver:26.06-py3-sdk /workspace/install/bin/image_client -m densenet_onnx -c 3 -s INCEPTION /workspace/images/mug.jpg\n# Inference should return the following\nImage '/workspace/images/mug.jpg':\n15.346230 (504) = COFFEE MUG\n13.224326 (968) = CUP\n10.422965 (505) = COFFEEPOTPlease read the QuickStart guide for additional information regarding this example. The quickstart guide also contains an example of how to launch Triton on CPU-only systems. New to Triton and wondering where to get started? Watch the Getting Started video.\nCheck out NVIDIA LaunchPad for free access to a set of hands-on labs with Triton Inference Server hosted on NVIDIA infrastructure.",
    "character_count": 841
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "58690d22-4864-4507-8003-6ae25c4170b0",
    "technology": "nvidia_triton_inference_server",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "nvidia_triton_inference_server/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "Check out NVIDIA LaunchPad for free access to a set of hands-on labs with Triton Inference Server hosted on NVIDIA infrastructure.\nSpecific end-to-end examples for popular models, such as ResNet, BERT, and DLRM are located in the NVIDIA Deep Learning Examples page on GitHub. The NVIDIA Developer Zone contains additional documentation, presentations, and examples.\nThe recommended way to build and use Triton Inference Server is with Docker images.\n- Install Triton Inference Server with Docker containers (Recommended)\n- Install Triton Inference Server without Docker containers\n- Build a custom Triton Inference Server Docker container\n- Build Triton Inference Server from source\n- Examples for deploying Triton Inference Server with Kubernetes and Helm on GCP, AWS, and NVIDIA FleetCommand\n- Secure Deployment Considerations",
    "character_count": 828
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "8427bbf5-0383-4104-a7ee-255c5704e6b9",
    "technology": "nvidia_triton_inference_server",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "nvidia_triton_inference_server/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "- Build Triton Inference Server from source\n- Examples for deploying Triton Inference Server with Kubernetes and Helm on GCP, AWS, and NVIDIA FleetCommand\n- Secure Deployment Considerations\nThe first step in using Triton to serve your models is to place one or more models into a model repository. Depending on the type of the model and on what Triton capabilities you want to enable for the model, you may need to create a model configuration for the model.\n- Add custom operations to Triton if needed by your model\n- Enable model pipelining with Model Ensemble and Business Logic Scripting (BLS)\n- Optimize your models setting scheduling and batching parameters and model instances.\n- Use the Model Analyzer tool to help optimize your model configuration with profiling\n- Learn how to explicitly manage what models are available by loading and unloading models\n- Read the Quick Start Guide to run Triton Inference Server on both GPU and CPU",
    "character_count": 942
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e58459d3-8ef1-440d-b1b8-088c70322155",
    "technology": "nvidia_triton_inference_server",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "nvidia_triton_inference_server/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "- Learn how to explicitly manage what models are available by loading and unloading models\n- Read the Quick Start Guide to run Triton Inference Server on both GPU and CPU\n- Triton supports multiple execution engines, called backends, including TensorRT, PyTorch, ONNX, OpenVINO, Python, and more\n- Not all the above backends are supported on every platform supported by Triton. Look at the Backend-Platform Support Matrix to learn which backends are supported on your target platform.\n- Learn how to optimize performance using the Performance Analyzer and Model Analyzer\n- Learn how to manage loading and unloading models in Triton\n- Send requests directly to Triton with the HTTP/REST JSON-based or gRPC protocols\nA Triton client application sends inference and other requests to Triton. The Python and C++ client libraries provide APIs to simplify this communication.\n- Review client examples for C++, Python, and Java\n- Configure HTTP and gRPC client options",
    "character_count": 961
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "99ad21be-3dc2-44a3-8ec4-00006d912abc",
    "technology": "nvidia_triton_inference_server",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "nvidia_triton_inference_server/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "- Review client examples for C++, Python, and Java\n- Configure HTTP and gRPC client options\n- Send input data (e.g. a jpeg image) directly to Triton in the body of an HTTP request without any additional metadata\nTriton Inference Server's architecture is specifically designed for modularity and flexibility\n- Customize Triton Inference Server container for your use case\n- Create custom backends in either C/C++ or Python\n- Create decoupled backends and models that can send multiple responses for a request or not send any responses for a request\n- Use a Triton repository agent to add functionality that operates when a model is loaded and unloaded, such as authentication, decryption, or conversion\n- Deploy Triton on Jetson and JetPack\n- Use Triton on AWS Inferentia",
    "character_count": 770
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "1809d677-8b4b-4f15-9d70-82ea75343784",
    "technology": "nvidia_triton_inference_server",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "nvidia_triton_inference_server/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "- Deploy Triton on Jetson and JetPack\n- Use Triton on AWS Inferentia\nContributions to Triton Inference Server are more than welcome. To contribute please review the contribution guidelines. If you have a backend, client, example or similar contribution that is not modifying the core of Triton, then you should file a PR in the contrib repo.\nWe appreciate any feedback, questions or bug reporting regarding this project. When posting issues in GitHub, follow the process outlined in the Stack Overflow document. Ensure posted examples are:\n- minimal – use as little code as possible that still produces the same problem\n- complete – provide all parts needed to reproduce the problem. Check if you can strip external dependencies and still show the problem. The less time we spend on reproducing problems the more time we have to fix it",
    "character_count": 835
  },
