    "text": "Connected Papers (What is Connected Papers?)\n\n\nLitmaps (What is Litmaps?)\n\n\nscite Smart Citations (What are Smart Citations?)\n\nCode, Data and Media Associated with this Article\nalphaXiv (What is alphaXiv?)\n\n\nCatalyzeX Code Finder for Papers (What is CatalyzeX?)\n\n\nDagsHub (What is DagsHub?)\n\n\nGotit.pub (What is GotitPub?)\n\n\nHugging Face (What is Huggingface?)\n\n\nScienceCast (What is ScienceCast?)\n\nDemos\nRecommenders and Search Tools\nInfluence Flower (What are Influence Flowers?)\n\n\nCORE Recommender (What is CORE?)",
    "character_count": 516
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "78c1411c-0a8c-41f2-a62a-14283b64cf6f",
    "technology": "one_shot_prompting",
    "source": "research_papers",
    "path": "one_shot_prompting/research_papers.txt",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "Hugging Face (What is Huggingface?)\n\n\nScienceCast (What is ScienceCast?)\n\nDemos\nRecommenders and Search Tools\nInfluence Flower (What are Influence Flowers?)\n\n\nCORE Recommender (What is CORE?)\n\narXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\narXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nBoth individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nHave an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? Learn more about arXivLabs.",
    "character_count": 710
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "044c3883-dc52-4f62-b895-da3c48b45e70",
    "technology": "openai_evals",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "openai_evals/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "You can now configure and run Evals directly in the OpenAI Dashboard. Get started →\nEvals provide a framework for evaluating large language models (LLMs) or systems built using LLMs. We offer an existing registry of evals to test different dimensions of OpenAI models and the ability to write your own custom evals for use cases you care about. You can also use your data to build private evals which represent the common LLMs patterns in your workflow without exposing any of that data publicly.\nIf you are building with LLMs, creating high quality evals is one of the most impactful things you can do. Without evals, it can be very difficult and time intensive to understand how different model versions might affect your use case. In the words of OpenAI's President Greg Brockman:",
    "character_count": 783
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e23ec922-c54d-4b0b-97a7-4a9aaf5babf5",
    "technology": "openai_evals",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "openai_evals/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "To run evals, you will need to set up and specify your OpenAI API key. After you obtain an API key, specify it using the OPENAI_API_KEY environment variable. Please be aware of the costs associated with using the API when running evals. You can also run and create evals using Weights & Biases.\nMinimum Required Version: Python 3.9\nOur evals registry is stored using Git-LFS. Once you have downloaded and installed LFS, you can fetch the evals (from within your local copy of the evals repo) with:\ncd evals\ngit lfs fetch --all\ngit lfs pullThis will populate all the pointer files under evals/registry/data.\nYou may just want to fetch data for a select eval. You can achieve this via:\ngit lfs fetch --include=evals/registry/data/${your eval}\ngit lfs pullIf you are going to be creating evals, we suggest cloning this repo directly from GitHub and installing the requirements using the following command:",
    "character_count": 902
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "a4b0c377-a794-4ad2-bc2a-b1d00faf3598",
    "technology": "openai_evals",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "openai_evals/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "git lfs pullIf you are going to be creating evals, we suggest cloning this repo directly from GitHub and installing the requirements using the following command:\npip install -e .Using -e, changes you make to your eval will be reflected immediately without having to reinstall.\nOptionally, you can install the formatters for pre-committing with:\npip install -e .[formatters]Then run pre-commit install to install pre-commit into your git hooks. pre-commit will now run on every commit.\nIf you want to manually run all pre-commit hooks on a repository, run pre-commit run --all-files. To run individual hooks use pre-commit run <hook_id>.\nIf you don't want to contribute new evals, but simply want to run them locally, you can install the evals package via pip:",
    "character_count": 759
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "810a6b4d-b8c6-41aa-8bd2-03fec2c6365c",
    "technology": "openai_evals",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "openai_evals/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "If you don't want to contribute new evals, but simply want to run them locally, you can install the evals package via pip:\npip install evalsYou can find the full instructions to run existing evals in run-evals.md and our existing eval templates in eval-templates.md. For more advanced use cases like prompt chains or tool-using agents, you can use our Completion Function Protocol.\nWe provide the option for you to log your eval results to a Snowflake database, if you have one or wish to set one up. For this option, you will further have to specify the SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT, SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE, SNOWFLAKE_USERNAME, and SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD environment variables.\nWe suggest getting starting by:\n- Walking through the process for building an eval: build-eval.md\n- Exploring an example of implementing custom eval logic: custom-eval.md\n- Writing your own completion functions: completion-fns.md\n- Review our starter guide for writing evals: Getting Started with OpenAI Evals",
    "character_count": 969
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b4e1d138-4475-49ba-9f32-ad0b3f69ea4b",
    "technology": "openai_evals",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "openai_evals/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "- Writing your own completion functions: completion-fns.md\n- Review our starter guide for writing evals: Getting Started with OpenAI Evals\nPlease note that we are currently not accepting evals with custom code! While we ask you to not submit such evals at the moment, you can still submit model-graded evals with custom model-graded YAML files.\nIf you think you have an interesting eval, please open a pull request with your contribution. OpenAI staff actively review these evals when considering improvements to upcoming models.\nDo you have any examples of how to build an eval from start to finish?\n- Yes! These are in the examplesfolder. We recommend that you also read throughbuild-eval.mdin order to gain a deeper understanding of what is happening in these examples.\nDo you have any examples of evals implemented in multiple different ways?",
    "character_count": 846
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d1299783-cdb5-4dfc-9ab4-26465ccf3716",
    "technology": "openai_evals",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "openai_evals/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "Do you have any examples of evals implemented in multiple different ways?\n- Yes! In particular, see evals/registry/evals/coqa.yaml. We have implemented small subsets of the CoQA dataset for various eval templates to help illustrate the differences.\nWhen I run an eval, it sometimes hangs at the very end (after the final report). What's going on?\n- This is a known issue, but you should be able to interrupt it safely and the eval should finish immediately after.\nThere's a lot of code, and I just want to spin up a quick eval. Help? OR,\nI am a world-class prompt engineer. I choose not to code. How can I contribute my wisdom?",
    "character_count": 627
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6394e75c-3e6f-4cfe-a32d-191293b9b390",
    "technology": "openai_evals",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "openai_evals/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "There's a lot of code, and I just want to spin up a quick eval. Help? OR,\nI am a world-class prompt engineer. I choose not to code. How can I contribute my wisdom?\n- If you follow an existing eval template to build a basic or model-graded eval, you don't need to write any evaluation code at all! Just provide your data in JSON format and specify your eval parameters in YAML. build-eval.md walks you through these steps, and you can supplement these instructions with the Jupyter notebooks in the examplesfolder to help you get started quickly. Keep in mind, though, that a good eval will inevitably require careful thought and rigorous experimentation!",
    "character_count": 654
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "032cd74a-e626-4736-af74-3eca91fef80a",
    "technology": "openai_evals",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "openai_evals/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "By contributing to evals, you are agreeing to make your evaluation logic and data under the same MIT license as this repository. You must have adequate rights to upload any data used in an eval. OpenAI reserves the right to use this data in future service improvements to our product. Contributions to OpenAI evals will be subject to our usual Usage Policies: https://platform.openai.com/docs/usage-policies.",
    "character_count": 408
  },
