    "path": "peft/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "| Submission Name | Accuracy |\n|---|---|\n| Human baseline (crowdsourced) | 0.897 |\n| Flan-T5 | 0.892 |\n| lora-t0-3b | 0.863 |\nTip\nThe bigscience/T0_3B model performance isn't optimized in the table above. You can squeeze even more performance out of it by playing around with the input instruction templates, LoRA hyperparameters, and other training related hyperparameters. The final checkpoint size of this model is just 19MB compared to 11GB of the full bigscience/T0_3B model. Learn more about the advantages of finetuning with PEFT in this blog post.\nQuantization is another method for reducing the memory requirements of a model by representing the data in a lower precision. It can be combined with PEFT methods to make it even easier to train and load LLMs for inference.\n- Learn how to finetune meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf with QLoRA and the TRL library on a 16GB GPU in the Finetune LLMs on your own consumer hardware using tools from PyTorch and Hugging Face ecosystem blog post.",
    "character_count": 987
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f39f7792-b1f7-49ba-aa09-4df6433d60db",
    "technology": "peft",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "- Learn how to finetune a openai/whisper-large-v2 model for multilingual automatic speech recognition with LoRA and 8-bit quantization in this notebook (see this notebook instead for an example of streaming a dataset).\nPEFT can help you save storage by avoiding full finetuning of models on each of downstream task or dataset. In many cases, you're only finetuning a very small fraction of a model's parameters and each checkpoint is only a few MBs in size (instead of GBs). These smaller PEFT adapters demonstrate performance comparable to a fully finetuned model. If you have many datasets, you can save a lot of storage with a PEFT model and not have to worry about catastrophic forgetting or overfitting the backbone or base model.\nPEFT is widely supported across the Hugging Face ecosystem because of the massive efficiency it brings to training and inference.",
    "character_count": 865
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f798dbcd-387f-4abe-aaeb-0b0e4c0446be",
    "technology": "peft",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "PEFT is widely supported across the Hugging Face ecosystem because of the massive efficiency it brings to training and inference.\nThe iterative diffusion process consumes a lot of memory which can make it difficult to train. PEFT can help reduce the memory requirements and reduce the storage size of the final model checkpoint. For example, consider the memory required for training a Stable Diffusion model with LoRA on an A100 80GB GPU with more than 64GB of CPU RAM. The final model checkpoint size is only 8.8MB!\n| Model | Full Finetuning | PEFT-LoRA | PEFT-LoRA with Gradient Checkpointing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CompVis/stable-diffusion-v1-4 | 27.5GB GPU / 3.97GB CPU | 15.5GB GPU / 3.84GB CPU | 8.12GB GPU / 3.77GB CPU |\nTip",
    "character_count": 731
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "700da326-c2e2-44b5-8271-64514aefba69",
    "technology": "peft",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "|---|---|---|---|\n| CompVis/stable-diffusion-v1-4 | 27.5GB GPU / 3.97GB CPU | 15.5GB GPU / 3.84GB CPU | 8.12GB GPU / 3.77GB CPU |\nTip\nTake a look at the examples/lora_dreambooth/train_dreambooth.py training script to try training your own Stable Diffusion model with LoRA, and play around with the smangrul/peft-lora-sd-dreambooth Space which is running on a T4 instance. Learn more about the PEFT integration in Diffusers in this tutorial.\nPEFT is directly integrated with Transformers. After loading a model, call add_adapter to add a new PEFT adapter to the model:\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nmodel = ... # transformers model\npeft_config = LoraConfig(...)\nmodel.add_adapter(peft_config, adapter_name=\"lora_1\")To load a trained PEFT adapter, call load_adapter:\nmodel = ... # transformers model\nmodel.load_adapter(<path-to-adapter>, adapter_name=\"lora_1\")And to switch between different adapters, call set_adapter:",
    "character_count": 915
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "1d7edcfc-5bfa-453b-9742-13b6d04e4e8a",
    "technology": "peft",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "text": "model = ... # transformers model\nmodel.load_adapter(<path-to-adapter>, adapter_name=\"lora_1\")And to switch between different adapters, call set_adapter:\nmodel.set_adapter(\"lora_2\")The Transformers integration doesn't include all the functionalities offered in PEFT, such as methods for merging the adapter into the base model.\nAccelerate is a library for distributed training and inference on various training setups and hardware (GPUs, TPUs, Apple Silicon, etc.). PEFT models work with Accelerate out of the box, making it really convenient to train really large models or use them for inference on consumer hardware with limited resources.\nPEFT can also be applied to training LLMs with RLHF components such as the ranker and policy. Get started by reading:\n- Fine-tune a Mistral-7b model with Direct Preference Optimization with PEFT and the TRL library to learn more about the Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) method and how to apply it to a LLM.",
    "character_count": 954
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "77ef5442-e344-441f-b09a-a48e3b7dbfc2",
    "technology": "peft",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 10,
    "text": "- Fine-tune a Mistral-7b model with Direct Preference Optimization with PEFT and the TRL library to learn more about the Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) method and how to apply it to a LLM.\n- Fine-tuning 20B LLMs with RLHF on a 24GB consumer GPU with PEFT and the TRL library, and then try out the gpt2-sentiment_peft.ipynb notebook to optimize GPT2 to generate positive movie reviews.\n- StackLLaMA: A hands-on guide to train LLaMA with RLHF with PEFT, and then try out the stack_llama/scripts for supervised finetuning, reward modeling, and RL finetuning.\nUse this Space or check out the docs to find which models officially support a PEFT method out of the box. Even if you don't see a model listed below, you can manually configure the model config to enable PEFT for a model. Read the New transformers architecture guide to learn how.\nIf you would like to contribute to PEFT, please check out our contribution guide.",
    "character_count": 925
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "173d9f40-219f-45f6-89c9-21471bd0e209",
    "technology": "peft",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "peft/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 11,
    "text": "If you would like to contribute to PEFT, please check out our contribution guide.\nTo use 🤗 PEFT in your publication, please cite it by using the following BibTeX entry.\n@Misc{peft,\ntitle = {{PEFT}: State-of-the-art Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning methods},\nauthor = {Sourab Mangrulkar and Sylvain Gugger and Lysandre Debut and Younes Belkada and Sayak Paul and Benjamin Bossan and Marian Tietz},\nhowpublished = {\\url{https://github.com/huggingface/peft}},\nyear = {2022}\n}",
    "character_count": 471
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "15f856d6-69de-4c14-aae5-9bc3afaff137",
    "technology": "peft",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "peft/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "PEFT documentation\nPEFT\nGet started\nGuides\nConfigurations and modelsIntegrations Memory Efficient TrainingModel mergingQuantizationCustom modelsAdapter injectionMixing PEFT methodstorch.compileContribute to PEFTTroubleshootingPEFT checkpoint format\nDistributed Training\nMethods\nAPI reference\nPEFT\n🤗 PEFT (Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning) is a library for efficiently adapting large pretrained models to various downstream applications without fine-tuning all of a model’s parameters because it is prohibitively costly. PEFT methods only fine-tune a small number of (extra) model parameters - significantly decreasing computational and storage costs - while yielding performance comparable to a fully fine-tuned model. This makes it more accessible to train and store large language models (LLMs) and other big models on consumer hardware.",
    "character_count": 838
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b7c5115b-6c9d-4015-8e8a-86883fb52c05",
    "technology": "peft",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "peft/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "PEFT is integrated with the Transformers, Diffusers, and Accelerate libraries to provide a faster and easier way to load, train, and use large models for inference.\nThere are numerous methods to \"adapt\" existing models, often extensively integrating into the model. PEFT can be thought of as a framework for arbitrary methods of model adaption (modifying weights, wrapping layers, manipulating KV-caches, ...) while also serving as a reference implementation for many fine-tuning methods.\nUpdate on GitHub",
    "character_count": 505
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "a9e08ee9-59fe-4977-9923-159f224e0ae0",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "api_reference",
    "path": "pinecone/api_reference.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "SDK versions are pinned to specific API versions.\nDatabase\nUse the Database API to store and query records in Pinecone Database. The following Pinecone SDKs support the Database API:CLI\nPython SDK\nNode.js SDK\nJava SDK\nGo SDK\nInference\nUse the Inference API to generate vector embeddings and rerank results using embedding models and reranking models hosted on Pinecone’s infrastructure. There are two ways to use the Inference API:- As a standalone service, through the Rerank documents and Generate vectors endpoints.\n- As an integrated part of database operations, through the Create an index with integrated embedding, Upsert text, and Search with text endpoints.",
    "character_count": 666
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "be51b92b-4212-45fd-943b-4931786931b9",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "pinecone/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Pinecone is the leading vector database for building accurate, performant AI\napplications at scale. We give models long-term memory and access to your data\nso they can reason over what they know and retrieve what they don't. Trusted by\n9,000+ companies and 800,000+ developers to power agents, search, and recommendation systems.\nWebsite · Documentation · Blog · Community\n- Official clients: Python, TypeScript, Go, Java\n- Examples: notebooks and sample apps\n- AI assistants: AI coding tools and Pinecone MCP\n- Infrastructure as code: Terraform provider\nNew to vector databases? Start with the quickstart.",
    "character_count": 606
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "ec1a059c-371c-4ada-8d3d-11c8284b1cb0",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "official_documentation",
    "path": "pinecone/official_documentation.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Get started\nDatabase quickstart\nBuild semantic search and knowledge retrieval into your agent or app\nAssistant quickstart\nCreate an AI assistant that answers questions about your proprietary data\nMarketplace quickstart\nPublish a no-code knowledge app from a template (public preview)\nBuild\nIDEs & CLIs\nUse Pinecone with Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Cursor, and other agentic tools\nMCP server\nConnect any MCP-compatible agent to Pinecone for search and index management\nAPI reference\nComprehensive details about the Pinecone APIs, SDKs, and architecture\nExamples\nNotebooks and sample apps with common AI patterns\nIntegrations\nThird-party integrations for LangChain, LlamaIndex, and more\nReleases\nWhat’s new in Pinecone",
    "character_count": 713
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "dac9bdf9-707d-43dd-bfaf-5ca79497ba59",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "July 2026\nDatabaseSearch\nFuzzy matching and substring search for full-text search\nFull-text search now supports two new ways to match text:- Fuzzy matching (typo tolerance) — In a query_stringsearch, append~to a term to match indexed terms within a small edit distance:body:(compxter~1)matchescomputer. Useterm~for an automatic distance based on term length, orterm~Nfor a fixed distance (Nis 0–2). Fuzzy works on existing indexes with no reindex. See Fuzzy matching.\n- Substring search with n-grams — Configure a text field for character n-gram tokenization to match on partial words, prefixes, and autocomplete-style queries. Add an ngramobject (min_gram,max_gram,prefix_only) to a field’sfull_text_searchconfig at index creation, then query it with ordinarytextorquery_stringsearches. See Substring search with n-grams.\n2026-01.alpha.General\nManage roles from your identity provider with SAML and SCIM",
    "character_count": 904
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b771752e-49e1-4e81-aaf8-805d1e6215e1",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "2026-01.alpha.General\nManage roles from your identity provider with SAML and SCIM\nOrganizations on the Enterprise plan with SSO can now manage Pinecone roles directly from their identity provider (IdP), instead of assigning them manually in Pinecone. Two modes are available:- SAML role management sets each user’s organization and project roles from a SAML attribute on every SSO login.\n- SCIM provisioning syncs members and their roles in real time as you add, update, deactivate, or remove them in your IdP.\npinecone:* role values and work with Okta and other SAML 2.0 / SCIM 2.0 providers. Configure them under Settings > Access > Identity provider in the Pinecone console. For more information, see Manage roles with Okta and SCIM provisioning with Okta.General\nExpanded role-based access controls (RBAC)",
    "character_count": 809
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "a7bcc6e3-507f-4a14-9f45-14c5042fc647",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "Pinecone now offers an expanded set of roles for managing access to organizations and projects. New Organization member and Project member roles grant more restricted access than the owner and manager roles, and projects now support granular editor and viewer roles (project, control plane, and data plane) that you can assign to users, service accounts, and API keys.To better communicate the access hierarchy, organization owners are now shown in each project’s member list. This replaces the separate project-role assignments that could fall out of sync with the access organization owners inherit to all projects. Effective permissions are unchanged.If you used the previous role model, the User role has been renamed to Manager: the organization-level User role is now Organization manager, and the project-level User role is now Project manager",
    "character_count": 850
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6f5d7e81-c06b-42e6-90d4-5523c5e1bd22",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": ". This is a name change only—permissions are unchanged and existing assignments are migrated automatically.You can assign and manage roles in the Pinecone console or programmatically with the Admin API. For more information, see Manage roles and access, Organization roles, and Project roles.DatabaseAPI",
    "character_count": 303
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6c1566c8-7e3c-40e7-86ab-39a8abb9c6c2",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "Public preview: Restore a backup to a different region\nYou can now restore a serverless backup into a different region on the same cloud provider. Use theunstable version of the create index endpoint, specifying the backup as source_backup_id within spec.serverless and the target region there. Restoring to a different cloud provider is not supported, and the feature is currently available through the REST API only.For details, see Restore to a different region.Inference\nCohere Rerank 4.0 Fast is generally available, and cohere-rerank-3.5 is deprecated",
    "character_count": 557
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f61102c5-7918-4514-9a3d-121e5adc91b6",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "Cohere Rerank 4.0 Fast is generally available, and cohere-rerank-3.5 is deprecated\ncohere-rerank-4-fast (Cohere Rerank 4.0 Fast) is now generally available as a reranking model on Pinecone. It succeeds cohere-rerank-3.5, improves relevance quality, and supports multiple rerank fields. Cohere Rerank 4.0 is hosted on Azure AI under the Global Standard deployment type, and requests may be processed in regions outside the United States.With this release, cohere-rerank-3.5 is deprecated:- Through July 31, 2026: cohere-rerank-3.5continues to serve requests as before.\n- August 1, 2026: requests to cohere-rerank-3.5are automatically served bycohere-rerank-4-fast.\n- Relevance scores differ. cohere-rerank-4-fastreturns different scores thancohere-rerank-3.5. If your application relies on hard-coded score thresholds, re-tune them againstcohere-rerank-4-fastbefore August 1.",
    "character_count": 874
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "4faf32e4-7a40-40c5-902b-c04ddf10306f",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "- Billing counts units differently. The per-unit rate is unchanged at $2.00 per 1,000 rerank units. One rerank unit covers a query plus up to 100 documents, with documents longer than ~500 tokens auto-chunked into ~500-token chunks that each count toward that 100. As a result, cohere-rerank-4-fastcan bill multiple units for a large request, whereascohere-rerank-3.5billed one unit per request.\ncohere-rerank-4-fast before August 1. For model details and parameters, see Reranking models.June 2026\nDatabaseBilling\nMulti-region and multi-cloud support on the Builder plan",
    "character_count": 571
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "8fe9b260-05d8-40db-b133-94f3b51d5082",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "cohere-rerank-4-fast before August 1. For model details and parameters, see Reranking models.June 2026\nDatabaseBilling\nMulti-region and multi-cloud support on the Builder plan\nThe Builder plan now supports serverless indexes in every generally available cloud region, the same regions available on the Standard and Enterprise plans. Previously, Builder indexes were limited to theus-east-1 region of AWS.Highlights:- All supported regions — Create Builder serverless indexes across AWS, GCP, and Azure, including us-west-2(Oregon),eu-west-1(Ireland),eu-central-1(Frankfurt),ap-southeast-1(Singapore),us-central1(Iowa),europe-west4(Netherlands), andeastus2(Virginia).\n- Lower latency and data residency — Deploy indexes closer to your application or in a region that meets your data residency requirements, without upgrading to Standard or Enterprise.\n- No pricing change — Builder remains a flat $20/month plan with no usage overages.",
    "character_count": 934
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f1b89c62-ee59-46f1-a669-90099f9b6596",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "- No pricing change — Builder remains a flat $20/month plan with no usage overages.\nus-east-1 region of AWS only. For the full list of supported regions, see Cloud regions. For plan quotas, see Database limits.Billing\nCheaper bulk imports and free import credit\nImporting from object storage is now cheaper: the bulk-import overage rate is $0.25/GB, down from $1/GB.In addition, Standard and Enterprise organizations now get a one-time $250 bulk import credit (1 TB), valid through July 31, 2026. Usage beyond the credit is billed at the new $0.25/GB rate. Builder and Free plans are not eligible.For details, see Understanding cost — Imports.May 2026\nMarketplace\nPublic preview: Pinecone Marketplace",
    "character_count": 700
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "45188028-4d13-4a9e-ad8f-cc81a5eab7d5",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "text": "Marketplace\nPublic preview: Pinecone Marketplace\nPinecone Marketplace is now in public preview. Marketplace lets you build, publish, and operate AI-powered knowledge applications on top of Pinecone, with a managed deployment lifecycle and end-user chat interface.Highlights:- Templates and connectors — Start from pre-built templates, connect data sources (Google Drive, manual upload), and configure knowledge processing with the Knowledge Agent Toolkit (KAT).\n- Multi-domain routing — Route end-user queries across multiple knowledge domains within a single deployment.\n- Evaluations and analytics — Run evaluations against your deployment and monitor usage with event logs.\n- Versioning and rollback — Publish deployment versions and roll back to previous configurations.\n- End-user experience — Authenticated chat interface with citations, visual components, and feedback collection.",
    "character_count": 887
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "976b9a5d-1e16-4b0f-bea9-68e19097673c",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 10,
    "text": "- End-user experience — Authenticated chat interface with citations, visual components, and feedback collection.\n- Increased Starter plan limits - The Starter plan is currently offering 1M input tokens per month (500K before promotion) to help explore Marketplace apps until June 30, 2026.\nBilling\nNew Builder plan\nThe Builder plan is now available at $20/month (flat). Builder is designed for individual developers who need higher limits than Starter without committing to usage-based pricing. Key differences from Starter:- 10 serverless indexes (up from 5)\n- 10 GB storage per organization\n- 100 namespaces per index (up from 20)\n- Prometheus and Datadog monitoring\nDatabaseSearch\nPublic preview: Full-text search",
    "character_count": 716
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "798328ed-9e63-4178-9cc2-6becf0bf3e20",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 11,
    "text": "- 10 GB storage per organization\n- 100 namespaces per index (up from 20)\n- Prometheus and Datadog monitoring\nDatabaseSearch\nPublic preview: Full-text search\nFull-text search is now in public preview. Full-text search uses a typed document model: you upsert data as JSON documents, declare ranking fields in a schema, and Pinecone indexes them accordingly. Schema field types:string with full_text_search (indexed for BM25 ranking), dense_vector, and sparse_vector. Any other fields you upsert are stored as metadata and automatically indexed for filtering — no schema declaration required.Highlights:- Four scoring methods via score_by:text(BM25),query_string(Lucene syntax, including cross-field boolean queries),dense_vector, andsparse_vector.\n- New data plane endpoints under /namespaces/{namespace}/documents/:upsert,search,fetch, anddelete.\n- New filter operator $match_phrasefor phrase matching against text fields, composable with anyscore_bymethod.",
    "character_count": 956
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "a171d5f6-a635-494f-a304-736f152131a5",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 12,
    "text": "- New filter operator $match_phrasefor phrase matching against text fields, composable with anyscore_bymethod.\n- Flexible deployment: on-demand read capacity (read_capacity.mode: \"OnDemand\") and dedicated read capacity (read_capacity.mode: \"Dedicated\") are both supported on managed (serverless) indexes.\n2026-01.alpha to access the feature.Database\nNew AWS regions for serverless indexes\nYou can now deploy serverless indexes in two new AWS regions:eu-central-1 (Frankfurt) and ap-southeast-1 (Singapore). Both regions are available on Standard and Enterprise plans. For the full list of supported regions, see Cloud regions.April 2026\nDatabase\nGeneral availability: Fetch by metadata",
    "character_count": 685
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "fb245618-3141-4e1f-8148-85f8e5c8c687",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 13,
    "text": "Database\nGeneral availability: Fetch by metadata\nThe Fetch by metadata operation is now generally available and recommended for production usage. Use a metadata filter expression to fetch matching records without knowing their IDs, and paginate withpaginationToken to retrieve result sets larger than 10,000 records per response.For more information, see Fetch records by metadata.Assistant\nUpsert files with custom IDs in Assistant\nPinecone Assistant now supports upserting files with user-provided file IDs, so you can create or replace a file by a stable custom identifier instead of relying on system-generated UUIDs. For more information, see File identifiers.As part of this update, upload, upsert, and delete operations now return an operation object that can be polled for status and progress. Assistant also includes new API endpoints to list and describe file operations.This update applies to the API only; SDK support is not yet available.Database",
    "character_count": 959
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "ad8c668e-705b-4c9c-b4ea-0c1493e380f7",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 14,
    "text": "General availability: Dedicated Read Nodes\nDedicated Read Nodes are now generally available and recommended for production usage on Standard and Enterprise plans. You can provision read hardware for large, high-throughput indexes that need predictable, low latency using the console and API.AssistantBilling\nPinecone Assistant usage-based pricing and monthly Starter limits",
    "character_count": 373
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "87111b5a-e1f4-4784-bfe2-27fb7ecd5cc5",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 15,
    "text": "Pinecone Assistant pricing is now fully usage-based. The hourly per-assistant fee has been removed. On Standard and Enterprise, you pay for what you use: ingestion (file uploads and updates on an assistant), storage, and chat, context, and evaluation tokens — with no base charge per assistant.For the Starter plan, Assistant included allowances are now monthly and reset each billing period (they are no longer all-time project totals). Starter includes 500,000 chat input tokens, 300,000 chat output tokens, 500,000 context retrieval tokens, and 1,000 ingestion units per month. When you upload files to an assistant, usage is measured in ingestion units (approximately one unit per chunk, ~400 tokens); multimodal PDF chunks account for more ingestion units per chunk than standard text on the same meter.Per-assistant file count limits have been removed for all plans",
    "character_count": 871
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6b0a56ca-76e3-4dde-8b53-87304f91b989",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 16,
    "text": ". Usage is governed by ingestion and storage allowances, file size and page limits, and rate limits instead of a cap on the number of stored documents.For details, see Pricing and limits and Pinecone pricing.March 2026",
    "character_count": 218
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c8865524-ad5c-42f1-b80b-76d162657cf2",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 17,
    "text": "General\nGeneral availability: platform and operations features\nThe following capabilities are now generally available and recommended for production usage:- Namespace creation — Create namespace API\n- Pinecone MCP server — Integrate AI agents with Pinecone\n- Assistant MCP server — Use an Assistant MCP server\n- Bulk metadata updates — Update metadata across multiple records\n- Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) — Configure CMEK\n- Data import from object storage (Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, Azure Blob Storage) — Import records\n- Audit logs — Configure audit logs\n- Admin API and service accounts (organization- and project-level) — Manage service accounts, Manage service accounts at the project level\n- Backups and restore (serverless indexes) — Back up an index, Restore an index\n- Pinecone Local (local development emulator) — Local development\n- Automated testing with Pinecone Local — Automated testing\n- Indexes with sparse vectors — Indexes with sparse vectors",
    "character_count": 980
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "da49c865-8c42-47d4-bad6-26148011adec",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 18,
    "text": "- Pinecone Local (local development emulator) — Local development\n- Automated testing with Pinecone Local — Automated testing\n- Indexes with sparse vectors — Indexes with sparse vectors\n- pinecone-sparse-english-v0— Sparse English embedding model\n- Prometheus monitoring (serverless indexes) — Monitor with Prometheus\n- Evaluate answers (metrics_alignment) — Evaluate answers\n- Manage storage integrations — Manage storage integrations\nFebruary 2026\nDatabase\nBYOC now available on AWS, GCP, and Azure\nBring Your Own Cloud (BYOC) is now available in public preview on AWS, GCP, and Azure. BYOC lets you run Pinecone’s data plane inside your own cloud account with a zero-access operating model — Pinecone never needs SSH, VPN, or inbound network access to your infrastructure.Deploy using a self-serve Pulumi-based setup wizard, with pull-based operations that execute locally in your cluster. Your vectors, metadata, and queries never leave your environment.Compliance",
    "character_count": 968
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d8d8d9c0-28f3-44ba-b75c-8a8b922cee41",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 19,
    "text": "HIPAA compliance add-on for Standard plan\nHIPAA compliance is now available as an optional add-on for Standard plan customers. For $190 per month, you get HIPAA-ready infrastructure, encrypted data storage, audit logging, enhanced security controls, and BAA execution and compliance documentation support.Full HIPAA compliance remains included with the Enterprise plan. To enable the add-on on the Standard plan, contact sales or see Understanding cost — HIPAA compliance add-on.January 2026\nAssistant\nClaude model deprecation for Assistant\nAnthropic has deprecated the Claude 3.5 Sonnet and Claude 3.7 Sonnet models. Pinecone Assistant automatically routes all chat requests that specifyclaude-3-5-sonnet or claude-3-7-sonnet to Claude Sonnet 4.5, which provides enhanced intelligence at the same price. No code changes are required.To update your code to explicitly use Claude Sonnet 4.5, set model: \"claude-sonnet-4-5\" in your chat requests. For more information, see Choose a model.Assistant",
    "character_count": 995
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "49ae6042-1d93-4782-be91-8f8705187a56",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 20,
    "text": "Pinecone Assistant node for n8n\nThe official Pinecone Assistant n8n node brings Assistant’s end-to-end RAG capabilities directly into n8n workflows, letting you connect any data source to AI-backed automation.For more information, see the Assistant quickstart for n8n.Assistant\nClaude Sonnet 4.5 now available for Assistant chat\nPinecone Assistant now supports Anthropic’s Claude Sonnet 4.5 model. To use this model, setmodel: \"claude-sonnet-4-5\" in your chat requests. In the Pinecone console, Claude Sonnet 4.5 is also available as a selection in the Chat model dropdown menu in the playground for each assistant.For more information, see Choose a model.API\nMetadata filter limit: 10,000 values per $in/$nin operator",
    "character_count": 718
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "a020c64b-133b-4d45-9e34-f8ca455cf459",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 21,
    "text": "Metadata filter limit: 10,000 values per $in/$nin operator\nPinecone now enforces a limit of 10,000 values per $in or $nin operator in metadata filter expressions. This limit helps ensure consistent query performance and protects shared infrastructure from excessive load caused by very large filters.Requests that exceed this limit will fail with a 400 - BAD_REQUEST error.If your application currently uses large $in filters (especially for access control), consider these approaches:- Namespace-based isolation (recommended): Create separate namespaces for each tenant instead of filtering by thousands of tenant IDs. This can also reduce query costs (queries on a 1 GB namespace cost 1 RU instead of 100 RUs for a 100 GB namespace with filtering).\n- Access control groups: Filter by organization, project, or role identifiers instead of individual user IDs.\n- Post-filter client-side: Retrieve a larger top K without filtering, then filter results in your application.\nAPI",
    "character_count": 975
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "8a875f41-fdfc-4c54-b0b9-93345f762185",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 22,
    "text": "- Post-filter client-side: Retrieve a larger top K without filtering, then filter results in your application.\nAPI\nRequest-per-second limits for data plane operations\nPinecone now enforces request-per-second rate limits on data plane operations (query, upsert, delete, and update) at the namespace level. These limits are set to 100 requests per second per namespace for all plans and provide protection against excessive request rates.Request-per-second limits are enforced in addition to existing read unit and write unit limits. If you exceed a request-per-second limit, you’ll receive a429 - TOO_MANY_REQUESTS error.For more information, see Database limits.API\nPagination support for fetch by metadata",
    "character_count": 706
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9c9fd180-5f7a-4d34-970d-5909ae5d44f0",
    "technology": "pinecone",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "pinecone/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 23,
    "text": "Pagination support for fetch by metadata\nThe Fetch by metadata operation now supports pagination, allowing you to fetch large result sets in multiple requests. Use thepaginationToken parameter to retrieve the next page of results.When there are more results available, the response includes a pagination object with a next token. Pass this token as the paginationToken parameter in subsequent requests to fetch the next page. When there are no more results, the response does not include a pagination object.For more information, see Fetch records by metadata.",
    "character_count": 560
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "03b205c4-761f-4aa6-80bd-ed3a39303661",
