    "source": "research_papers",
    "path": "self_consistency/research_papers.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Computer Science > Computation and Language\n[Submitted on 21 Mar 2022 (v1), last revised 7 Mar 2023 (this version, v4)]\nTitle:Self-Consistency Improves Chain of Thought Reasoning in Language Models",
    "character_count": 197
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "8c0b8060-b6ff-4bdc-9d68-ec3d3b0bdab3",
    "technology": "self_consistency",
    "source": "research_papers",
    "path": "self_consistency/research_papers.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "View PDFAbstract:Chain-of-thought prompting combined with pre-trained large language models has achieved encouraging results on complex reasoning tasks. In this paper, we propose a new decoding strategy, self-consistency, to replace the naive greedy decoding used in chain-of-thought prompting. It first samples a diverse set of reasoning paths instead of only taking the greedy one, and then selects the most consistent answer by marginalizing out the sampled reasoning paths. Self-consistency leverages the intuition that a complex reasoning problem typically admits multiple different ways of thinking leading to its unique correct answer. Our extensive empirical evaluation shows that self-consistency boosts the performance of chain-of-thought prompting with a striking margin on a range of popular arithmetic and commonsense reasoning benchmarks, including GSM8K (+17.9%), SVAMP (+11.0%), AQuA (+12.2%), StrategyQA (+6.4%) and ARC-challenge (+3.9%).\nSubmission history",
    "character_count": 974
  },
