    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "print(\"Reference: {0}\\nModel prediction: {1}\".format(top_doc[\"title\"] + \"\\n\" + top_doc[\"text\"], preds[0].outputs[0].text))Output:\nReference: Overfitting\nIn statistics, overfitting is \"the production of an analysis that corresponds too closely or exactly to a particular set of data, and may therefore fail to fit additional data or predict future observations reliably\". An overfitted model is a statistical model that contains more parameters than can be justified by the data. The essence of overfitting is to have unknowingly extracted some of the residual variation (i.e., the noise) as if that variation represented underlying model structure. Underfitting occurs when a statistical model cannot adequately capture the underlying structure of the data. An under-fitted model is a model where some parameters or terms that would appear in a correctly specified model are",
    "character_count": 874
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "56dbc5e9-f033-435e-a2fc-57b40e848396",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "Model prediction: [Relevant]Overfitting occurs when a model has too many parameters relative to the amount of data it has been trained on, leading it to memorize the training data too closely and perform poorly on new, unseen data.[Fully supported][Utility:5]</s>\nThe retriever system properly retrieves necessary document and generate fully grounded output.\nNote that this demo uses a smaller corpus and Self-RAG with the full inference algorithm. For a full evaluation, you either need to set up a retriever or download our retrieved results. Please follow instructions at Inference.\nBy default, we use Contriever as our retrieval component.\nDownload preprocessed passage data used in DPR.\ncd retrieval_lm\nwget https://dl.fbaipublicfiles.com/dpr/wikipedia_split/psgs_w100.tsv.gz\nThen, download the generated passages. We use Contriever-MSMARCO\nwget https://dl.fbaipublicfiles.com/contriever/embeddings/contriever-msmarco/wikipedia_embeddings.tar",
    "character_count": 947
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "30d1373f-bcd4-4f56-a4e3-44180b5f1f61",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "text": "Then, download the generated passages. We use Contriever-MSMARCO\nwget https://dl.fbaipublicfiles.com/contriever/embeddings/contriever-msmarco/wikipedia_embeddings.tar\nYou can run passage retrieval by running the command below.\ncd retrieval_lm\npython passage_retrieval.py \\\n--model_name_or_path facebook/contriever-msmarco --passages psgs_w100.tsv \\\n--passages_embeddings \"wikipedia_embeddings/*\" \\\n--data YOUR_INPUT_FILE \\\n--output_dir YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE \\\n--n_docs 20\nYour input file should be either a json or jsonl. Each instance must contain either question or instruction, which will be used as a query during retrieval.\nYou can generate embeddings for your own data by running the following command. (The script is adapted from the Contriever repository.) Note that generating embeddings from a large-scale corpus (>10M docs) can take time, and we recommend running it on multiple GPUs.\ncd retrieval_lm\nfor i in {0..3}; do\nexport CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=${i}",
    "character_count": 959
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e362b4aa-10b7-48ff-9f47-d929f1f4348a",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 10,
    "text": "cd retrieval_lm\nfor i in {0..3}; do\nexport CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=${i}\npython generate_passage_embeddings.py --model_name_or_path facebook/contriever-msmarco \\\n--output_dir YOUR_OUTPUT_DIR \\\n--passages YOUR_PASSAGE_DATA --shard_id ${i} --num_shards 4 > ./log/nohup.my_embeddings.${i} 2>&1 &\nSelf-RAG trains two models, Critic and Generator, both of which expand token vocabularies with reflection tokens and are trained with the standard next token prediction objective.\n- Step 1: Critic Data Creation: Generating Critic training data with GPT4.\n- Step 2: Critic Training: Training a Critic with new special tokens.\n- Step 3: Generator Data Creation: Generating Generator training data using Critic and Retriever.\n- Step 4: Generator Training: Training a Generator with new special tokens.\nAlternatively, you can download our training data consisting of 150K instances here.",
    "character_count": 872
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "0b615f58-12d5-424e-8fbc-d159846b831c",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 11,
    "text": "- Step 4: Generator Training: Training a Generator with new special tokens.\nAlternatively, you can download our training data consisting of 150K instances here.\nWe collect training data from GPT-4. The scripts to call GPT-4 for each special token type are available at data_creation/critic.\nAlternatively, you can download our training data at here.\nOnce you create or download training data, run the command below to fine-tune Llama2-7B on critic training.\ncd data_creation\ntorchrun --nproc_per_node=2 \\\n--master_port=2568 train_special_tokens.py \\\n--model_name_or_path meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-hf \\\n--data_path PATH_TO_TRAIN_DATA_FILE \\\n--bf16 True \\\n--output_dir PATH_TO_CRITIC_MODEL \\\n--num_train_epochs 3 \\\n--per_device_train_batch_size 1 --per_device_eval_batch_size 1 \\\n--gradient_accumulation_steps 8 \\\n--evaluation_strategy \"no\" \\\n--save_strategy \"steps\" \\\n--save_steps 300 \\\n--save_total_limit 1 \\\n--learning_rate 2e-5 \\\n--weight_decay 0. \\\n--warmup_ratio 0.01 \\",
    "character_count": 969
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "27908183-2235-4bb7-b5f5-1b9224fc10a7",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 12,
    "text": "--gradient_accumulation_steps 8 \\\n--evaluation_strategy \"no\" \\\n--save_strategy \"steps\" \\\n--save_steps 300 \\\n--save_total_limit 1 \\\n--learning_rate 2e-5 \\\n--weight_decay 0. \\\n--warmup_ratio 0.01 \\\n--lr_scheduler_type \"cosine\" \\\n--logging_steps 10 \\\n--fsdp \"full_shard auto_wrap\"\nThe code to create Generator training data is under generator_data_creation. See the instructions at README.md.\nAlternatively, you can download our training data at HuggingFace dataset or here\nFor generator training, we use DeepSpeed to make training more efficient. You can run training by running the script below, after setting the training data path.\ncd retrieval_lm\nbash script_finetune_7b.sh\nFor 13B model training, use training_13b. We use 8 A100 with 40 GRAM for 7B model training and 4 a100 with 80 GB GRAM for 13B training. 7B should fit 1-2 A100 although training can be slow.\nFor the task evaluation conducted in the paper, please download the data here.",
    "character_count": 944
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f536664d-4f30-4d94-8c58-669c84a1c6d1",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 13,
    "text": "For the task evaluation conducted in the paper, please download the data here.\nEach file already comes with retrieved documents, so if you don't want to run a retriever as a part of inference, you can simply load the retrieved docs at contexts.\nBelow, we describe Self-RAG and baselines.\n- Short-form: run evaluation for short-form generation.\n- Long-form: run evaluations for long-form generations.\nAs we typically retrieve only once for a short-form generation task, we provide an easy-to-run evaluation script that leverages pre-given documents retrieved by Contriever offline. See the individual command below.\npython run_short_form.py \\\n--model_name selfrag/selfrag_llama2_7b \\\n--input_file eval_data/popqa_longtail_w_gs.jsonl \\\n--mode MODE --max_new_tokens 100 \\\n--threshold 0.2 \\\n--output_file YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE \\\n--metric match --ndocs 10 --use_groundness --use_utility --use_seqscore \\\n--dtype half",
    "character_count": 906
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9e7f2dc1-ea79-42da-9e6a-780ed3202296",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 14,
    "text": "--mode MODE --max_new_tokens 100 \\\n--threshold 0.2 \\\n--output_file YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE \\\n--metric match --ndocs 10 --use_groundness --use_utility --use_seqscore \\\n--dtype half\nmode specifies the inference time mode among ['adaptive_retrieval', 'no_retrieval', 'always_retrieve'].\n- adaptive_retrievalretrieves given the- thresholdor Self-RAG prediction\n- no_retrievaldisables retrieval at inference time\n- always_retrievealways retrieves.\nFor 13B, you may have an OOM issue if you use a single GPU with 24 GRAM. You can run inference on multiple GPUs by setting --world_size.\npython run_short_form.py \\\n--model_name selfrag/selfrag_llama2_7b \\\n--input_file eval_data/arc_challenge_processed.jsonl \\\n--max_new_tokens 50 --threshold 0.2 \\\n--output_file OUTPUT_FILE_NAME \\\n--metric match --ndocs 5 --use_groundness --use_utility --use_seqscore \\\n--task arc_c\npython run_short_form.py \\\n--model_name selfrag/selfrag_llama2_7b \\\n--input_file eval_data/health_claims_processed.jsonl \\\n--max_new_tokens 50 \\",
    "character_count": 997
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "0695dd6f-93b8-40de-a460-26ba967ae445",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 15,
    "text": "--task arc_c\npython run_short_form.py \\\n--model_name selfrag/selfrag_llama2_7b \\\n--input_file eval_data/health_claims_processed.jsonl \\\n--max_new_tokens 50 \\\n--threshold 0.2 --output_file OUTPUT_FILE_NAME \\\n--metric match --ndocs 5 \\\n--use_groundness --use_utility --use_seqscore \\\n--task fever\nFor long-form QA, you can either run evaluations with a retrieval model or with pre-given passages. Currently, we are working on reducing run-time memory requirements (DPR / Contriever with the whole English Wikipedia Embeddings requires 100 GB RAM) speeding up for long-form generations, and releasing the inference code using a small set of initial retrieved documents first (~20).\nNote: Our current implementation is specifically designed for evaluations of target task datasets. We are planning to update our code base to make the interface more simple and easier to use. We will announce it when we release another version.\nFor ASQA, please run the following command,",
    "character_count": 967
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c3d89676-a1bc-45c3-ad0a-7cf37ae1d19e",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 16,
    "text": "For ASQA, please run the following command,\npython run_long_form_static.py \\\n--model_name selfrag/selfrag_llama2_7b \\\n--ndocs 5 --max_new_tokens 300 --threshold 0.2 \\\n--use_grounding --use_utility --use_seqscore \\\n--task asqa --input_file eval_data/asqa_eval_gtr_top100.json \\\n--output_file YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE_NAME --max_depth 7 --mode always_retrieve \\\nFor FactScore,\npython run_long_form_static.py \\\n--model_name selfrag/selfrag_llama2_7b \\\n--ndocs 5 --max_new_tokens 300 --threshold 0.2 \\\n--use_grounding --use_utility --use_seqscore \\\n--task factscore --input_file eval_data/factscore_unlabeled_alpaca_13b_retrieval.jsonl \\\n--output_file YOUR_OUTPUT_FILE_NAME --max_depth 7 \\\nThere are several key parameters related to the inference of Self-RAG.\n- w_rel(default 1.0):- w_relcontrols the emphasis on the- isRel(a critique token on whether retrieved passages are relevant or not) token probability during beam search.",
    "character_count": 918
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "15222165-2929-4ad3-83a7-66c73076ba9f",
    "technology": "self_rag",
    "source": "github_repository",
    "path": "self_rag/github_repository.txt",
    "chunk_index": 17,
    "text": "- w_rel(default 1.0):- w_relcontrols the emphasis on the- isRel(a critique token on whether retrieved passages are relevant or not) token probability during beam search.\n- w_sup(default 1.0):- w_supcontrols the emphasis on the- isSup(a critique token on whether the generation is supported by the document or not) token probability during beam search.\n- w_use(default 0.5):- w_usecontrols the emphasis on the- isUse(a critique token on overall quality) token probability during beam search.\n- threshold(default 0.2): this threshold controls the frequency of adaptive retrieval.\n- max_depth(default 6): this corresponds to- Tin the paper, which defines the maximum depth of search.\n- beam_width(default 2): this controls the size of the beam in the segment-level beam search.\nFor more details, please refer to the details (Section 3.3) and analysis (Section 5) in our paper.\nFor long-form evaluations, set up external libraries or repositories to run evaluations.",
    "character_count": 962
