  {
    "chunk_id": "7c66d45e-ee6e-413e-b464-c6662a97ff59",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "text": "Releases: huggingface/sentence-transformers\nRelease list\nv5.6.1 - Flash Attention Fix for XLM-R and RoBERTa Models\nv5.6.1 - Flash Attention Fix for XLM-R and RoBERTa Models\nThis patch release fixes silently degraded embeddings for RoBERTa-family models when flash attention is requested with transformers v5, notably every XLM-R based multilingual embedding model (BAAI/bge-m3, intfloat/multilingual-e5-large, etc.). The bug affected v5.5.0, v5.5.1, and v5.6.0.\nInstall this version with\n# Training + Inference\npip install sentence-transformers[train]==5.6.1\n# Inference only, use one of:\npip install sentence-transformers==5.6.1\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx-gpu]==5.6.1\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx]==5.6.1\npip install sentence-transformers[openvino]==5.6.1\n# Multimodal dependencies (optional):\npip install sentence-transformers[image]==5.6.1\npip install sentence-transformers[audio]==5.6.1\npip install sentence-transformers[video]==5.6.1\n# Or combine as needed:",
    "character_count": 984
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "cc5325a4-0397-4a70-b8b2-7a8c529f870f",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "text": "pip install sentence-transformers[image]==5.6.1\npip install sentence-transformers[audio]==5.6.1\npip install sentence-transformers[video]==5.6.1\n# Or combine as needed:\npip install sentence-transformers[train,onnx,image]==5.6.1Fix position_ids offsetting for RoBERTa-family models when flattening inputs (#3879)\nSince v5.5.0, the Transformer module flattens text-only batches into one packed sequence when flash attention is requested, skipping all padding overhead for a notable performance improvement. The position_ids of that packed sequence restart at 0 for every text, which is correct for the vast majority of models. RoBERTa-family architectures however compute positions as padding_idx + 1 + n for the n-th token, so every token read a position embedding shifted by padding_idx + 1 (usually 2). Nothing crashes, the embeddings are just silently worse.\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n# An affected configuration: flash attention with an XLM-R based model",
    "character_count": 984
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "cff5a2db-ba0c-4b41-a02b-3f8f7683ad90",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "text": "from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n# An affected configuration: flash attention with an XLM-R based model\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n\"BAAI/bge-m3\",\nmodel_kwargs={\"attn_implementation\": \"flash_attention_2\"},\n)Measured on BAAI/bge-m3:\n| Evaluation | padded | packed, 0-based positions | packed, with this fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| stsb test Spearman | 0.8485 | 0.7239 | 0.8485 |\n| NanoBEIR mean nDCG@10 | 0.6041 | 0.5414 | 0.6050 |",
    "character_count": 450
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c6b270d9-8afb-4b79-bfd3-03d6618562cb",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 3,
    "text": "| Evaluation | padded | packed, 0-based positions | packed, with this fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| stsb test Spearman | 0.8485 | 0.7239 | 0.8485 |\n| NanoBEIR mean nDCG@10 | 0.6041 | 0.5414 | 0.6050 |\nThe quality loss recovers exactly once the offset is applied. The fix scans the loaded model's modules once for an int padding_idx stored next to a learned position_embeddings table, and offsets the packed position_ids when that pair is found. An audit of transformers finds 16 architectures with that pair (roberta, xlm_roberta, xlm_roberta_xl, camembert, roberta_prelayernorm, xmod, data2vec_text, longformer, luke, ibert, mpnet, markuplm, lilt, layoutlmv3, esm, and pp_doclayout_v2), all offset by exactly padding_idx + 1, and no 0-based or rotary architecture matches.",
    "character_count": 769
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "44d4d5cf-52a0-43cf-a736-c85bb22d6865",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 4,
    "text": "You are only affected if you encoded text with flash attention requested on transformers v5 with a RoBERTa-family checkpoint. The default padded path (e.g. sdpa) was never affected, and neither were MPNet models like all-mpnet-base-v2 despite mpnet appearing in the audit: transformers does not support flash attention for MPNet at all. If you did index a corpus with such a configuration, re-encode it after upgrading: pre-fix embeddings score notably worse and do not mix with post-fix embeddings.\nWhat's Changed\n- [ci] Exclude librosa/numba/llvmlite on Python 3.13 by @tomaarsen in #3835\n- [tests] Skip bf16 + Windows + CPU forwards, as they can WindowsError on torch 2.13 by @tomaarsen in #3863\n- 🚨 Fix position_ids offsetting for RoBERTa-family models when flattening inputs by @tomaarsen in #3879\nFull Changelog: v5.6.0...v5.6.1\nv5.6.0 - Fixes for Causal LM Rerankers, Hard-Negative Mining, and More",
    "character_count": 905
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d1f8e2ee-22a8-4f5c-80ec-70bb36b61108",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 5,
    "text": "Full Changelog: v5.6.0...v5.6.1\nv5.6.0 - Fixes for Causal LM Rerankers, Hard-Negative Mining, and More\nThis minor version is a correctness- and robustness-focused release. It fixes a silent scoring bug for causal-LM rerankers, corrects several hard-negative mining and GIST loss edge cases, restores TSDAE on transformers v5, and adds Apple Silicon (MPS) support for the cached losses.\nThe headline fix affects chat-template models that read the final token position, i.e. causal-LM rerankers (like Qwen3-Reranker) and last-token-pooling embedders: when an over-long input was truncated, the chat template's trailing suffix (e.g. the assistant prefill the model scores from) was silently dropped, producing wrong scores with no error. There's also a forward-looking deprecation: loading local custom code without trust_remote_code=True now warns, and will require it from v6.0.\nInstall this version with\n# Training + Inference\npip install sentence-transformers[train]==5.6.0",
    "character_count": 974
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6f55d522-f0f7-474d-a683-551c4284c1e8",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 6,
    "text": "Install this version with\n# Training + Inference\npip install sentence-transformers[train]==5.6.0\n# Inference only, use one of:\npip install sentence-transformers==5.6.0\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx-gpu]==5.6.0\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx]==5.6.0\npip install sentence-transformers[openvino]==5.6.0\n# Multimodal dependencies (optional):\npip install sentence-transformers[image]==5.6.0\npip install sentence-transformers[audio]==5.6.0\npip install sentence-transformers[video]==5.6.0\n# Or combine as needed:\npip install sentence-transformers[train,onnx,image]==5.6.0Fixed silently wrong scores when truncation drops chat-template suffixes (#3787)",
    "character_count": 661
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "7ba7bdf6-effa-4950-a6a2-b5e54c5e5149",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 7,
    "text": "# Or combine as needed:\npip install sentence-transformers[train,onnx,image]==5.6.0Fixed silently wrong scores when truncation drops chat-template suffixes (#3787)\nChat-template models render the full conversation to a flat string before tokenizing, so when the rendered input is longer than the tokenizer's model_max_length, the tokenizer truncates it from the right and drops the template's trailing suffix: the fixed tokens a template appends after the content, e.g. a prompt, instruction, [/INST], or a trailing EOS. For models that read the final token position, this silently corrupted the result:\n- causal-LM rerankers (e.g. Qwen/Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B) score a pair from the last token'syes/nologits, and\n- last-token-pooling embedders read the final hidden state.\nWhen the suffix was truncated away, that final position landed mid-document instead of after the prefill, so the score or embedding came from the wrong place.",
    "character_count": 927
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "cda935ff-cc1b-4fa3-89c0-be98770f0f78",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 8,
    "text": "When the suffix was truncated away, that final position landed mid-document instead of after the prefill, so the score or embedding came from the wrong place.\nTransformer.preprocess now detects when truncation drops the suffix and splices it back onto the tail of each truncated row. Because the fix lives in the shared base Transformer, it applies across SentenceTransformer, CrossEncoder, and SparseEncoder. It's enabled by default and saved to the model configuration. Pass processing_kwargs={\"chat_template\": {\"restore_suffix\": False}} to opt back into raw truncation.\nHard-negative mining and GIST loss correctness (#3821, #3817, #3816)\nA trio of correctness and scalability fixes for hard-negative mining and the GIST losses:",
    "character_count": 731
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "cc30e771-3f61-411d-996a-c6942ebfe45a",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 9,
    "text": "Hard-negative mining and GIST loss correctness (#3821, #3817, #3816)\nA trio of correctness and scalability fixes for hard-negative mining and the GIST losses:\n- Sign-independent relative margin: mine_hard_negatives(relative_margin=...)and themargin_strategy=\"relative\"branch ofGISTEmbedLoss/CachedGISTEmbedLossused a multiplicative threshold (positive * (1 - margin)) that only behaves correctly when the positive-pair similarity is positive. When that similarity was negative, the threshold moved the wrong way and let through false negatives: candidates more similar to the anchor than the true positive. The threshold is nowpositive - |positive| * margin, identical to before for positive scores but correct for negative ones.",
    "character_count": 729
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "88ba68c9-2cb7-40e7-bf51-b2412d31b3c4",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 10,
    "text": "- Distributed positive masking in the GIST losses: with gather_across_devices=Trueand a non-zeromargin, the false-negative suppression mask protected the wrong columns on ranks beyond the first (it ignored the per-rank offset into the gathered batch), which set the true positive's logit to-infand produced a+infloss. The mask now accounts for the cross-rank offset, so multi-GPU GIST training stays finite.\n- Memory-bounded mining without FAISS: mine_hard_negatives(use_faiss=False)(the default) materialized the full(queries × corpus)similarity matrix at once, which could OOM on large corpora. It now batches over the query axis (controlled byfaiss_batch_size, default 16384), bounding peak memory while producing identical results.\nTSDAE weight tying restored on transformers v5 (#3781)",
    "character_count": 790
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "deaac7b7-c183-408f-b32e-e7dbc3f5f5ae",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 11,
    "text": "TSDAE weight tying restored on transformers v5 (#3781)\ntransformers v5 removed the private PreTrainedModel._tie_encoder_decoder_weights helper that DenoisingAutoEncoderLoss (TSDAE) used to tie its separate encoder and decoder. As a stopgap, v5.5 raised a RuntimeError for the default tie_encoder_decoder=True on transformers >= 5.0.0, effectively breaking TSDAE there unless you pinned an older transformers or disabled tying. TSDAE now ships its own tying routine that shares storage between encoder and decoder, so it works on both transformers <5 and >=5 with the default settings.\nDeprecation: loading local custom code without trust_remote_code (#3807)",
    "character_count": 657
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "1ee092ee-0adf-4393-8bf0-94c2bf3aec77",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 12,
    "text": "Deprecation: loading local custom code without trust_remote_code (#3807)\nSentence Transformers has historically treated any local model directory as implicitly trusted: local custom code (e.g. modeling_*.py) loaded even with trust_remote_code=False, unlike transformers. This discrepancy might be unexpected, so loading local custom code this way now emits a FutureWarning, and from v6.0 it will require trust_remote_code=True like in transformers.\nApple Silicon (MPS) support (#3812, #3818)\nTwo fixes for training on Apple Silicon:\n- Cached losses on MPS: CachedMultipleNegativesRankingLossandCachedGISTEmbedLosscrashed at construction on MPS because theirRandContextused a CUDA-only RNG path. They now run on MPS with deterministic replay preserved.",
    "character_count": 751
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e6c6990e-fb2c-4771-bbdc-ede5ca9e84ea",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 13,
    "text": "- Legacy fit path and SparseEncodersparsity on MPS: the legacymodel.fit(..., use_amp=True)path hard-coded CUDA's AMPGradScaler/autocast, andSparseEncodersparsity statistics calledto_sparse_csr(), which is unimplemented on MPS. Both now work on Apple Silicon.\nBug Fixes\n- Cast learning-to-rank loss logits to float32 in #3800: the listwise learning-to-rank losses scatter the model's logits into a float32 matrix, which crashed with a dtype mismatch when the model itself was in bf16/fp16 and trained without bf16=True/fp16=True(with those enabled, autocast outputs float32 logits, so the common path was unaffected). Logits are now upcast to float32 in the loss.",
    "character_count": 662
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "46c5d7f9-7c41-4187-a90d-97556de406a1",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 14,
    "text": "- Don't override device_mapplacement with thedeviceargument in #3823: loading withmodel_kwargs={\"device_map\": ...}previously placed the backbone viaaccelerate, then immediately moved it to the default device, defeatingdevice_map. It now keeps the backbone in place (moving the other modules onto its device), and warns if bothdeviceanddevice_mapare passed.\n- Collapse single-key multimodal dicts to the bare modality in #3779: a single-key input like {\"image\": img}was classified as a combined modality and rejected by models that support only that one modality (e.g. BGE-VL), with a self-contradicting error. It is now treated as the bare\"image\"input, which unblocks vision-retrieval benchmarks like MTEB that pass{\"image\": ...}.",
    "character_count": 730
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f4d3ac33-5c47-4dc8-8529-cbd3a5b4ee11",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 15,
    "text": "- Clarify unsupported-modality error messages in #3792: mixed or combined multimodal inputs on models that can't fuse modalities produced confusing errors (e.g. Modality 'message' is not supported). The errors are now scenario-specific and suggest what to do, such as encoding each modality separately.\n- Guard distributed APIs in get_device_namein #3798: on PyTorch builds wheretorch.distributedis present but unavailable (some ROCm and CPU-only builds),get_device_name()crashed withAttributeError: module 'torch.distributed' has no attribute 'is_initialized'. It now checksis_available()first, across all distributed call sites.\n- Fix OpenVINO static quantization for optimum-intel 2.0 / OpenVINO 2026 in #3814: export_static_quantized_openvino_modeldefaulted its calibration dataset to the bare id\"glue\", which the stricter Hub repo-id validation now rejects. The default is now the namespaced\"nyu-mll/glue\".\nExamples, Documentation, and Notebooks",
    "character_count": 950
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "63c3bed9-8dc3-4175-bcd1-018a556eba83",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 16,
    "text": "Examples, Documentation, and Notebooks\nA batch of example and documentation modernization, mostly migrating example scripts off deprecated datasets script-loaders and bare ids onto maintained Hugging Face datasets so they run on datasets 4.x:\n- Fix example datasets that crash on datasets4.x in #3782 (e.g.quora,nq_open,yahoo_answers_topics).\n- Migrate the MS MARCO examples to datasetsin #3783.\n- Migrate the multilingual parallel-sentences data-prep scripts to datasetsin #3784.\n- Migrate the Quora semantic-search and clustering examples to datasetsin #3785.\n- Modernize the AugSBERT data-augmentation STSb scripts in #3806, also fixing the QQP cross-domain script to use the quora-duplicateslabels it previously ignored.\n- Refresh the CLIP / image-search notebooks in #3780, and modernize the CLIP training notebook in #3805.\n- Fix a reStructuredText table in the ContrastiveTensionLossdocstring in #3788, and add a low-VRAM hardware note to the efficiency docs in #3802.",
    "character_count": 975
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "4a55ba96-24a1-4e31-8c46-cfc0592cb478",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 17,
    "text": "- Fix a reStructuredText table in the ContrastiveTensionLossdocstring in #3788, and add a low-VRAM hardware note to the efficiency docs in #3802.\n- Fix package build warnings and errors in #3809, and a batch of Sphinx doc-build problems in #3811.\nAll Changes\n- [chore] Increment dev version by @tomaarsen in #3775\n- [fix] Collapse single-key multimodal dicts to bare modality by @tomaarsen in #3779\n- chore: enable Dependabot weekly GitHub Actions bumps by @hf-dependantbot-rollout[bot] in #3786\n- Bump the actions group with 3 updates by @dependabot[bot] in #3790\n- Fix example datasets that crash on datasets 4.x by @omkar-334 in #3782\n- CLIP Notebooks Refresh by @lbourdois in #3780\n- fix TSDAE ...\nv5.5.1 - Small Multimodal patch\nThis patch release fixes a small quirk with multimodal inference when using single-key multimodal inputs like model.encode({\"image\": ...}).\nInstall this version with\n# Training + Inference\npip install sentence-transformers[train]==5.5.1",
    "character_count": 970
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "db7ca91a-a03e-4617-a103-ce7a18635f2b",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 18,
    "text": "Install this version with\n# Training + Inference\npip install sentence-transformers[train]==5.5.1\n# Inference only, use one of:\npip install sentence-transformers==5.5.1\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx-gpu]==5.5.1\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx]==5.5.1\npip install sentence-transformers[openvino]==5.5.1\n# Multimodal dependencies (optional):\npip install sentence-transformers[image]==5.5.1\npip install sentence-transformers[audio]==5.5.1\npip install sentence-transformers[video]==5.5.1\n# Or combine as needed:\npip install sentence-transformers[train,onnx,image]==5.5.1Bug fixed\nPreviously, inference like model.encode({\"image\": ...}) or model.encode([{\"image\": ...}, ...]) would be inferred as the (\"image\",) modality, which differed from the inferred modality of \"image\" for just model.encode(my_image) or model.encode([my_image, my_image_2, ...]).",
    "character_count": 862
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "beeb2034-fe0a-44f7-807a-496cac6d4dd5",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 19,
    "text": "This results in confusing errors if the model doesn't have a modality_config mapping for (\"image\",) in addition to \"image\", so now a single-key multimodal dict is collapsed to the bare modality (just \"image\" in this example).\nThis affected this code:\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nmodel = SentenceTransformer('BAAI/BGE-VL-base', trust_remote_code=True)\nembedding = model.encode({\"image\": \"https://huggingface.co/datasets/huggingface/documentation-images/resolve/main/blog/ettin-reranker/mteb_ndcg10_all-MiniLM-L6-v2.png\"})\nprint(embedding.shape)Which previously failed as the model only implements a path for \"text\", \"image\", and (\"image\", \"text\").\nAll Changes\n- [fix] Collapse single-key multimodal dicts to bare modality by @tomaarsen in #3779\nFull Changelog: v5.5.0...v5.5.1\nv5.5.0 - Training Agent Skill, EmbedDistillLoss, and ADRMSELoss",
    "character_count": 865
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "7d69d062-878a-43f2-9a4a-4c74bbad4bc7",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 20,
    "text": "All Changes\n- [fix] Collapse single-key multimodal dicts to bare modality by @tomaarsen in #3779\nFull Changelog: v5.5.0...v5.5.1\nv5.5.0 - Training Agent Skill, EmbedDistillLoss, and ADRMSELoss\nThis release ships the train-sentence-transformers Agent Skill, adds two new training losses, and brings a long list of robustness and correctness fixes.\nThe new train-sentence-transformers Agent Skill lets AI coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, ...) drive end-to-end training and fine-tuning across all three model types. EmbedDistillLoss is a new embedding-level knowledge distillation loss for SentenceTransformer: it aligns a student model's embeddings with pre-computed teacher embeddings, an alternative to the score-based distillation provided by MarginMSELoss and DistillKLDivLoss. ADRMSELoss is a new listwise learning-to-rank loss for CrossEncoder from the Rank-DistiLLM paper. encode() and predict() also gain a per-call processing_kwargs override, and more.",
    "character_count": 981
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "7bb05a57-655c-47dd-80c2-0a9503bc7d0a",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 21,
    "text": "Install this version with\n# Training + Inference\npip install sentence-transformers[train]==5.5.0\n# Inference only, use one of:\npip install sentence-transformers==5.5.0\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx-gpu]==5.5.0\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx]==5.5.0\npip install sentence-transformers[openvino]==5.5.0\n# Multimodal dependencies (optional):\npip install sentence-transformers[image]==5.5.0\npip install sentence-transformers[audio]==5.5.0\npip install sentence-transformers[video]==5.5.0\n# Or combine as needed:\npip install sentence-transformers[train,onnx,image]==5.5.0The train-sentence-transformers Agent Skill (#3752)\nIf you use an AI coding agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, ...), you can now install the train-sentence-transformers Agent Skill and ask your agent to fine-tune a model on your data:\nhf skills add train-sentence-transformers # installs under ./.agents/skills/",
    "character_count": 914
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "40bcc77b-c5b4-48d8-8a03-23a6b0150ed4",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 22,
    "text": "hf skills add train-sentence-transformers # installs under ./.agents/skills/\nhf skills add train-sentence-transformers --global # installs under ~/.agents/skills/\nhf skills add train-sentence-transformers --claude # also symlinks into .claude/skills/The skill gives the agent curated, version-aware guidance for training SentenceTransformer (bi-encoder), CrossEncoder (reranker), and SparseEncoder/SPLADE models, covering base model selection, loss and evaluator choice, hard-negative mining, distillation, LoRA, Matryoshka, multilingual training, static embeddings, plus a set of production-ready training template scripts. Then you can prompt your agent with things like:\n\"Train a multilingual sentence-transformer on Dutch legal pairs.\"\n\"Fine-tune a cross-encoder reranker on\n(question, answer)pairs from my dataset, mine hard negatives, and push to my Hub repo.\"\"Train a German sparse embedding model with high sparsity.\"\n\"Can you train a static embedding model on 100k code triplets?\"",
    "character_count": 989
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "bcb139b9-2f95-4a30-b9d4-b08da92224da",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 23,
    "text": "\"Can you train a static embedding model on 100k code triplets?\"\nThe skill lives in the repository under skills/train-sentence-transformers/ and is mirrored to the huggingface/skills marketplace on each release.\nNew loss: EmbedDistillLoss (#3665)",
    "character_count": 245
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c2366e9b-7870-4abf-8d99-7ce62d5a3611",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 24,
    "text": "New loss: EmbedDistillLoss (#3665)\nIntroduces EmbedDistillLoss (Kim et al., 2023), an embedding-level knowledge distillation loss for SentenceTransformer. Rather than distilling teacher scores (MarginMSELoss, DistillKLDivLoss), it directly aligns the student's sentence_embedding with a pre-computed teacher embedding passed via the dataset's label column. The comparison uses a configurable distance_metric, one of \"cosine\" (the default), \"l2\", or \"mse\". When the student and teacher dimensions differ, pass projection_dim=<teacher_dim> to add a learnable projection from the student's embedding space into the teacher's. That projection lives on the loss rather than on the saved model, so use loss.save_projection(...) / loss.load_projection(...) to reuse it across stages (e.g. like done in Arkam et al. for Jina v5). As part of this change, MSELoss is now a thin subclass of EmbedDistillLoss with distance_metric=\"mse\", and also gains the optional projection_dim argument.",
    "character_count": 977
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c46f0b1f-109f-41a8-b254-d15ce9597a69",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 25,
    "text": "from datasets import Dataset\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer, SentenceTransformerTrainer\nfrom sentence_transformers.sentence_transformer.losses import EmbedDistillLoss\nstudent_model = SentenceTransformer(\"microsoft/mpnet-base\")\nteacher_model = SentenceTransformer(\"all-mpnet-base-v2\")\ntrain_dataset = Dataset.from_dict({\n\"sentence\": [\"It's nice weather outside today.\", \"He drove to work.\"],\n})\n# Pre-compute teacher embeddings once and store them as the `label` column\ndef add_teacher_embeddings(batch):\nreturn {\"label\": teacher_model.encode(batch[\"sentence\"]).tolist()}\ntrain_dataset = train_dataset.map(add_teacher_embeddings, batched=True)\nloss = EmbedDistillLoss(student_model, distance_metric=\"cosine\")\n# If the student and teacher dimensions differ, add a learnable projection:\n# loss = EmbedDistillLoss(student_model, distance_metric=\"cosine\", projection_dim=768)\ntrainer = SentenceTransformerTrainer(\nmodel=student_model,\ntrain_dataset=train_dataset,\nloss=loss,\n)",
    "character_count": 994
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "8b9a7225-30ab-433f-947b-a9aa7418bc72",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 26,
    "text": "# loss = EmbedDistillLoss(student_model, distance_metric=\"cosine\", projection_dim=768)\ntrainer = SentenceTransformerTrainer(\nmodel=student_model,\ntrain_dataset=train_dataset,\nloss=loss,\n)\ntrainer.train()See the updated model distillation examples and the loss overview for more.\nNew loss: ADRMSELoss for Cross Encoders (#3690)\nIntroduces ADRMSELoss (Approx Discounted Rank Mean Squared Error), a listwise learning-to-rank loss for CrossEncoder from the Rank-DistiLLM paper (Schlatt et al., ECIR 2025). It computes a differentiable approximation of each document's rank via pairwise sigmoids and minimizes the nDCG-discounted squared error against the true ranks derived from the labels. It expects listwise inputs: a (query, [doc1, ..., docN]) pair plus a [score1, ..., scoreN] label list per sample (binary or continuous labels, variable document counts allowed). It's designed for LLM-distillation reranking, where the per-document scores come from a strong LLM's ordering.",
    "character_count": 975
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "0a9adde3-da22-43c5-8aa1-e7b64d0f8e16",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 27,
    "text": "from datasets import Dataset\nfrom sentence_transformers import CrossEncoder, CrossEncoderTrainer\nfrom sentence_transformers.cross_encoder.losses import ADRMSELoss\nmodel = CrossEncoder(\"microsoft/mpnet-base\")\ntrain_dataset = Dataset.from_dict({\n\"query\": [\"What are pandas?\", \"What is the capital of France?\"],\n\"docs\": [\n[\"Pandas are a kind of bear.\", \"Pandas are kind of like fish.\"],\n[\"The capital of France is Paris.\", \"Paris is the capital of France.\", \"Paris is quite large.\"],\n],\n\"scores\": [[0.95, 0.1], [0.98, 0.92, 0.2]],\n})\nloss = ADRMSELoss(model)\ntrainer = CrossEncoderTrainer(\nmodel=model,\ntrain_dataset=train_dataset,\nloss=loss,\n)\ntrainer.train()There's a full MS MARCO example at training_ms_marco_adrmse.py. Note that LambdaLoss generally remains the strongest loss in the listwise family. See the Cross Encoder loss overview for guidance on picking a loss.\nPer-call processing_kwargs override (#3753)",
    "character_count": 914
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "e17743c7-efb8-4c88-ac12-ac245734d1f4",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 28,
    "text": "Per-call processing_kwargs override (#3753)\nSentenceTransformer.encode() / encode_query() / encode_document(), SparseEncoder.encode(), CrossEncoder.predict(), and model.preprocess() now accept a processing_kwargs argument that overrides the processor/tokenizer kwargs configured at construction time, for a single call. It has the same nested structure as the processing_kwargs constructor argument (top-level keys text, audio, image, video, common, chat_template) and is shallow-merged on top of the instance-level settings, so you can override just one setting (e.g. max_length) and leave the rest intact.\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\n# Override processor kwargs (e.g. max_length, truncation) for this call only:\nembeddings = model.encode(\n[\"a short text\", \"a much longer text that you want truncated more aggressively ...\"],\nprocessing_kwargs={\"text\": {\"max_length\": 256, \"truncation\": True}},",
    "character_count": 969
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d90b2a36-8a72-425b-8023-2d88527c857f",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 29,
    "text": "embeddings = model.encode(\n[\"a short text\", \"a much longer text that you want truncated more aggressively ...\"],\nprocessing_kwargs={\"text\": {\"max_length\": 256, \"truncation\": True}},\n)This is especially handy for vision-language models, where you can change the image resolution per call, e.g. model.encode(images, processing_kwargs={\"image\": {\"max_pixels\": 256 * 256}}).\nSmaller Features\n- Allow CrossEncodermodule stacks that don't start with aTransformer, and recognize a trailingDense(module_output_name=\"scores\")as the scoring head, by @tomaarsen in #3742:num_labelsnow reads that head'sout_features, andmodel.config/model.modelreturnNonewhen there's no underlying transformers model.\n- Infer that a model is an IR model on its generated model card when an InformationRetrievalEvaluator/NanoBEIREvaluator(or their sparse variants) was used during training, by @tomaarsen in #3741: the usage snippet then showsencode_query/encode_document, even without IR prompt names or aRouterarchitecture.",
    "character_count": 995
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "febee35f-130d-4918-97d1-9d3dd5d1f1ac",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 30,
    "text": "- Warn at model-load time when the installed transformersversion is too old to honoruse_bidirectional_attention/is_causalflags in a model's config (e.g. forgoogle/embeddinggemma-300m), rather than silently ignoring them, by @tomaarsen in #3726.\nBug Fixes\n- Use the first non-pad token for CLS pooling with left-padding tokenizers by @tomaarsen in #3767: pooling_mode=\"cls\"previously returned the embedding at position 0, which is a[PAD]token for left-padded inputs (common with decoder-only models), silently producing incorrect sentence embeddings. It now ...\nv5.4.1 - Numpy string arrays\nThis patch release allows encode() and predict() to accept 1D numpy string arrays as inputs.\nInstall this version with\n# Training + Inference\npip install sentence-transformers[train]==5.4.1\n# Inference only, use one of:\npip install sentence-transformers==5.4.1\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx-gpu]==5.4.1\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx]==5.4.1\npip install sentence-transformers[openvino]==5.4.1",
    "character_count": 999
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "3a083f45-fbfc-4fec-b8f6-84ba730fc6d8",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 31,
    "text": "pip install sentence-transformers==5.4.1\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx-gpu]==5.4.1\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx]==5.4.1\npip install sentence-transformers[openvino]==5.4.1\n# Multimodal dependencies (optional):\npip install sentence-transformers[image]==5.4.1\npip install sentence-transformers[audio]==5.4.1\npip install sentence-transformers[video]==5.4.1\n# Or combine as needed:\npip install sentence-transformers[train,onnx,image]==5.4.1Numpy string/object arrays as batches (#3720)\nencode() and predict() now correctly recognize 1D numpy string/object arrays as batches rather than singular inputs. Previously, something like model.encode(df[\"text\"].to_numpy()) was silently treated as a single input and produced incorrect output. 1D numpy arrays with dtype.kind in (\"U\", \"O\") are now unpacked like lists, and 2D+ arrays are treated as batches of pairs (for CrossEncoder).\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer",
    "character_count": 964
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "6f0eaa09-ab3d-4f8a-937c-066e0979e37e",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 32,
    "text": "import numpy as np\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\n# Previously treated as one input; now correctly encoded as 3 separate texts\nembeddings = model.encode(np.array([\"first\", \"second\", \"third\"]))\nprint(embeddings.shape)\n# (3, 384)For CrossEncoder, a 1D numpy string array is still treated as a single [query, document] pair to match the existing list behavior, while a 2D array of shape (N, 2) is a batch of N pairs.\nSafer activation function loading in Dense (#3714)\nThe Dense module stores its activation function as a dotted import path in its saved config (e.g. \"torch.nn.modules.activation.Tanh\"), which was then resolved via import_from_string whenever the module was loaded. Because any importable Python callable could be referenced, a maliciously crafted config.json on the Hub could trigger arbitrary imports at model load time.",
    "character_count": 906
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9a8d28ad-2261-4e68-9a9e-daf8a8d584ea",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 33,
    "text": "The loader now only resolves activation functions whose import path starts with torch.. Anything else is skipped with a warning and replaced by the default activation (Tanh). To load a model with a custom (non-torch) activation function, opt in explicitly with trust_remote_code=True:\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n# Torch-provided activations load as before\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"some/model-with-torch-activation\")\n# Non-torch activations now require explicit opt-in\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"some/model-with-custom-activation\", trust_remote_code=True)This mirrors the opt-in trust model already used by transformers for custom code, and ensures untrusted model repositories cannot smuggle arbitrary imports through the Dense activation config.\nWhat's Changed\n- [tests] Fix test_trainer_prompts for SE and ST after prompt handling moved into Transformer.preprocess by @tomaarsen in #3710\n- [chore] Increment dev version after v5.4 release by @tomaarsen in #3711",
    "character_count": 996
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "9b0c03f3-c55e-4994-afb2-6c017a601b9b",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 34,
    "text": "- [chore] Increment dev version after v5.4 release by @tomaarsen in #3711\n- [docs] No revision needed anymore for nvidia nemotron by @tomaarsen in #3712\n- [chore] Replace evaluation_strategy with eval_strategy in a few more places by @tomaarsen in #3713\n- [security] Only load activation functions starting with 'torch' in the Dense module by @tomaarsen in #3714\n- [fix] Treat numpy string/object arrays as batches in encode/predict by @tomaarsen in #3720\nFull Changelog: v5.4.0...v5.4.1\nv5.4.0 - Multimodal Embeddings and Reranking, Modular CrossEncoder, Flash Attention Input Flattening",
    "character_count": 588
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "0d3cae97-efdf-4629-841a-00fb10b5437c",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 35,
    "text": "Full Changelog: v5.4.0...v5.4.1\nv5.4.0 - Multimodal Embeddings and Reranking, Modular CrossEncoder, Flash Attention Input Flattening\nThis large release introduces first-class multimodal support for both SentenceTransformer and CrossEncoder, making it easy to compute embeddings and rerank across text, images, audio, and video. The CrossEncoder class has been fully modularized, allowing for generative rerankers (CausalLM-based models) via a new LogitScore module. Flash Attention 2 now automatically skips padding for text-only inputs, providing significant speedups & memory reductions, especially when input lengths vary.\nBlog post: Multimodal Embedding & Reranker Models with Sentence Transformers: a walkthrough of the new multimodal capabilities with some practical examples.",
    "character_count": 782
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "4a80e2ec-a07e-413c-934f-2c50b0ef5beb",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 36,
    "text": "Blog post: Multimodal Embedding & Reranker Models with Sentence Transformers: a walkthrough of the new multimodal capabilities with some practical examples.\nMigration guide: Migrating from v5.x to v5.4+: covers updated import paths, renamed parameters, and other softly breaking changes with deprecation warnings. Note that there are no hard deprecations, all existing code should continue to work with warnings at worst.\nInstall this version with\n# Training + Inference\npip install sentence-transformers[train]==5.4.0\n# Inference only, use one of:\npip install sentence-transformers==5.4.0\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx-gpu]==5.4.0\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx]==5.4.0\npip install sentence-transformers[openvino]==5.4.0\n# Multimodal dependencies (optional):\npip install sentence-transformers[image]==5.4.0\npip install sentence-transformers[audio]==5.4.0\npip install sentence-transformers[video]==5.4.0\n# Or combine as needed:",
    "character_count": 944
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "717bd0d0-4e10-49e1-bdcc-bdfaacd08e21",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 37,
    "text": "pip install sentence-transformers[image]==5.4.0\npip install sentence-transformers[audio]==5.4.0\npip install sentence-transformers[video]==5.4.0\n# Or combine as needed:\npip install sentence-transformers[train,onnx,image]==5.4.0Multimodal Embeddings with SentenceTransformer (#3554)\nSentenceTransformer now natively supports vision-language models (VLMs) and other multimodal architectures. You can encode and compare across text, images, audio, videos, or combinations of these, with automatic modality detection and preprocessing. Models advertise which modalities they support via the new model.modalities property and model.supports() method.\nUsing a pretrained multimodal embedding model\nfrom PIL import Image\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n\"Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B\",\nmodel_kwargs={\"attn_implementation\": \"flash_attention_2\", \"torch_dtype\": \"bfloat16\"},\nprocessor_kwargs={\"min_pixels\": 28 * 28, \"max_pixels\": 600 * 600},",
    "character_count": 978
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "45ab7f87-31e2-4eba-ad97-3420ae9adfaa",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 38,
    "text": "\"Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B\",\nmodel_kwargs={\"attn_implementation\": \"flash_attention_2\", \"torch_dtype\": \"bfloat16\"},\nprocessor_kwargs={\"min_pixels\": 28 * 28, \"max_pixels\": 600 * 600},\nrevision=\"refs/pr/23\",\n)\n# Check supported modalities\nprint(model.modalities)\n# ['text', 'image', 'video', 'message']\nprint(model.supports(\"image\"))\n# True\n# Encode text\ntext_embeddings = model.encode([\"A photo of a cat\", \"A pollinator on a flower\"])\n# Encode images (PIL images, file paths, or URLs all work)\nimage_embeddings = model.encode([\nImage.open(\"cat.jpg\"),\n\"https://example.com/flower.jpg\",\n])\n# Encode mixed text+image inputs\nmultimodal_embeddings = model.encode([\n{\"text\": \"Describe this image\", \"image\": Image.open(\"cat.jpg\")},\n])\n# Compute cross-modal similarity\nsimilarity = model.similarity(text_embeddings, image_embeddings)Building multimodal models with Router",
    "character_count": 862
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d7e251d9-6a79-4881-89a9-bc9da10efe84",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 39,
    "text": "])\n# Compute cross-modal similarity\nsimilarity = model.similarity(text_embeddings, image_embeddings)Building multimodal models with Router\nYou can also compose separate encoders for different modalities using the new Router module. Unlike the single-backbone VLM approach, Router lets you combine any existing text and image encoders and route inputs based on detected modality:\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nfrom sentence_transformers.sentence_transformer.modules import Dense, Pooling, Router, Transformer\n# Text encoder: MiniLM with mean pooling, projected to 768 dims to match image encoder\ntext_encoder = Transformer(\"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\ntext_pooling = Pooling(text_encoder.get_embedding_dimension(), pooling_mode=\"mean\")\ntext_projection = Dense(text_encoder.get_embedding_dimension(), 768)\n# Image encoder: SigLIP outputs pooled embeddings directly\nimage_encoder = Transformer(\"google/siglip2-base-patch16-224\")",
    "character_count": 962
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "364abf8d-35db-4ac7-b4ef-75c7c267a028",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 40,
    "text": "text_projection = Dense(text_encoder.get_embedding_dimension(), 768)\n# Image encoder: SigLIP outputs pooled embeddings directly\nimage_encoder = Transformer(\"google/siglip2-base-patch16-224\")\n# Route inputs to the appropriate encoder based on detected modality\nrouter = Router(\nsub_modules={\n\"text\": [text_encoder, text_pooling, text_projection],\n\"image\": [image_encoder],\n},\n)\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(modules=[router])\n# Text and image inputs are automatically routed to the correct encoder\ntext_embeddings = model.encode([\"A photo of a cat\"])\nimage_embeddings = model.encode([\"https://example.com/cat.jpg\"])\nsimilarity = model.similarity(text_embeddings, image_embeddings)Multimodal Reranking with CrossEncoder (#3554)",
    "character_count": 723
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "89cc5bc0-0b2f-4176-bd9f-a0e9a32091fd",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 41,
    "text": "image_embeddings = model.encode([\"https://example.com/cat.jpg\"])\nsimilarity = model.similarity(text_embeddings, image_embeddings)Multimodal Reranking with CrossEncoder (#3554)\nCrossEncoder now supports multimodal inputs for reranking, enabling cross-modal scoring of query-document pairs where either side can be text, images, audio, video, or mixed-modality content. This works with both generative rerankers (CausalLM-based, via the new LogitScore module) and encoder-based models. See the pretrained multimodal rerankers for models you can use right away.\nfrom sentence_transformers import CrossEncoder\n# Load a multimodal reranker\nmodel = CrossEncoder(\"Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Reranker-2B\", revision=\"refs/pr/11\")\n# Rank text documents against an image query (or vice versa)\nresults = model.rank(\nquery=\"https://example.com/product.jpg\",\ndocuments=[\"A red sneaker\", \"A blue dress\", \"A leather bag\"],\n)Two training approaches are provided in the multimodal training examples:",
    "character_count": 968
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f8551a1e-f602-4f68-8457-336addc4e190",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 42,
    "text": "results = model.rank(\nquery=\"https://example.com/product.jpg\",\ndocuments=[\"A red sneaker\", \"A blue dress\", \"A leather bag\"],\n)Two training approaches are provided in the multimodal training examples:\n- Any-to-Any + LogitScore: Uses the full causal LM to generate a single token, scoring via log-odds of \"1\" vs \"0\".\n- Feature Extraction + Pooling + Dense: More memory-efficient alternative that skips the LM head.\nModular CrossEncoder Architecture (#3554)\nCrossEncoder has been fully modularized, inheriting from BaseModel (which is a torch.nn.Sequential). You can now inspect, customize, and compose module chains, just like SentenceTransformer. See the custom models guide for full details.\nfrom sentence_transformers import CrossEncoder\nmodel = CrossEncoder(\"Qwen/Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B\", revision=\"refs/pr/11\")\nprint(model)\n\"\"\"\nCrossEncoder(\n(0): Transformer({'transformer_task': 'text-generation', ...})\n(1): LogitScore({'true_token_id': 9693, 'false_token_id': 2152, ...})\n)",
    "character_count": 976
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d8e6d301-1c67-4176-bf69-9127372f6363",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 43,
    "text": "print(model)\n\"\"\"\nCrossEncoder(\n(0): Transformer({'transformer_task': 'text-generation', ...})\n(1): LogitScore({'true_token_id': 9693, 'false_token_id': 2152, ...})\n)\n\"\"\"Generative reranker support\nThanks to the modular architecture, generative rerankers like mixedbread-ai/mxbai-rerank-base-v2 now work out of the box. These models ship with a modules.json that configures the Transformer + LogitScore chain automatically:\nfrom sentence_transformers import CrossEncoder\nmodel = CrossEncoder(\"mixedbread-ai/mxbai-rerank-base-v2\")\nscores = model.predict([\n(\"How many people live in Berlin?\", \"Berlin had a population of 3,520,031 in 2022.\"),\n(\"How many people live in Berlin?\", \"Berlin is well known for its museums.\"),\n])\n# array([ 9. , -0.5], dtype=float32)Module chain patterns\nThe Transformer module now supports multiple task types that determine how the underlying model is loaded and what outputs it produces:",
    "character_count": 914
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "58db524c-8fa1-4138-9549-9fd8a7ce3632",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 44,
    "text": "])\n# array([ 9. , -0.5], dtype=float32)Module chain patterns\nThe Transformer module now supports multiple task types that determine how the underlying model is loaded and what outputs it produces:\n- \"sequence-classification\": Loads via- AutoModelForSequenceClassification, returns classification logits directly.\n- \"text-generation\": Loads via- AutoModelForCausalLM, returns raw logits from the language model head.\n- \"any-to-any\": Loads via- AutoModelForMultimodalLM(transformers v5+), for multimodal causal LMs that accept interleaved image/text inputs.\n- \"feature-extraction\": Loads via- AutoModel(no task-specific head), returns hidden states.\nVarious module chains are possible now, here's some common ones:\n-\nEncoder-based (Sequence Classification): A single Transformermodule withtransformer_task=\"sequence-classification\", the traditional BERT/RoBERTa approach. This was previously the only option for CrossEncoder models.\n-",
    "character_count": 932
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "f3d6d4d7-b2b3-498f-9170-6883584690c1",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 45,
    "text": "-\nCausalLM-based (Text Generation + LogitScore): For generative rerankers (Qwen, Llama, mxbai-rerank-v2, etc.), a Transformerwithtransformer_task=\"text-generation\"followed by aLogitScoremodule that computeslogit[\"yes\"] - logit[\"no\"]at the last token position. For multimodal rerankers,transformer_task=\"any-to-any\"is used instead.\n-\nFeature Extraction + Pooling + Dense: A memory-efficient alternative that uses the base model without LM head, pools the last token, and projects to a single score via a Dense layer.\nWhen loading a model without a modules.json, CrossEncoder automatically selects the right chain: if the architecture ends with ForCausalLM, it uses text-generation + LogitScore (with \"yes\"/\"no\" tokens); otherwise it uses sequence-classification. You can also construct custom module chains explicitly:\nfrom sentence_transformers import CrossEncoder\nfrom sentence_transformers.cross_encoder.modules import Transformer, LogitScore",
    "character_count": 944
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "c1c9bff4-c2eb-4cf5-a34a-8beeb5c4f292",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 46,
    "text": "from sentence_transformers import CrossEncoder\nfrom sentence_transformers.cross_encoder.modules import Transformer, LogitScore\ntransformer = Transformer(\"Qwen/Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B\", transformer_task=\"text-generation\", revision=\"refs/pr/11\")\ntrue_id = transformer.tokenizer.convert_tokens_to_ids(\"1\")...v5.3.0 - Improved Contrastive Learning, New Losses, and Transformers v5 Compatibility\nThis minor version brings several improvements to contrastive learning: MultipleNegativesRankingLoss now supports alternative InfoNCE formulations (symmetric, GTE-style) and optional hardness weighting for harder negatives. Two new losses are introduced, GlobalOrthogonalRegularizationLoss for embedding space regularization and CachedSpladeLoss for memory-efficient SPLADE training. The release also adds a faster hashed batch sampler, fixes GroupByLabelBatchSampler for triplet losses, and ensures full compatibility with the latest Transformers v5 versions.\nInstall this version with\n# Training + Inference",
    "character_count": 996
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "d7cb2e2c-ecda-46b7-ab81-cb9d4513fa38",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 47,
    "text": "Install this version with\n# Training + Inference\npip install sentence-transformers[train]==5.3.0\n# Inference only, use one of:\npip install sentence-transformers==5.3.0\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx-gpu]==5.3.0\npip install sentence-transformers[onnx]==5.3.0\npip install sentence-transformers[openvino]==5.3.0Updated MultipleNegativesRankingLoss (a.k.a. InfoNCE)\nMultipleNegativesRankingLoss received two major upgrades: support for alternative InfoNCE formulations from the literature, and optional hardness weighting to up-weight harder negatives.\nSupport other InfoNCE variants (#3607)\nMultipleNegativesRankingLoss now supports several well-known contrastive loss variants from the literature through new directions and partition_mode parameters. Previously, this loss only supported the standard forward direction (query → doc). You can now configure which similarity interactions are included in the loss:",
    "character_count": 917
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "577c726d-6a69-4aaa-88e8-b0cb3fe93477",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 48,
    "text": "- \"query_to_doc\"(default): For each query, its matched document should score higher than all other documents.\n- \"doc_to_query\": The symmetric reverse — for each document, its matched query should score higher than all other queries.\n- \"query_to_query\": For each query, all other queries should score lower than its matched document.\n- \"doc_to_doc\": For each document, all other documents should score lower than its matched query.\nThe partition_mode controls how scores are normalized: \"joint\" computes a single softmax over all directions, while \"per_direction\" computes a separate softmax per direction and averages the losses.\nThese combine to reproduce several loss formulations from the literature:\nStandard InfoNCE (default, unchanged behavior):\nloss = MultipleNegativesRankingLoss(model)\n# equivalent to directions=(\"query_to_doc\",), partition_mode=\"joint\"Symmetric InfoNCE (Günther et al. 2024) — adds the reverse direction so both queries and documents are trained to find their match:",
    "character_count": 994
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "13e796ef-6216-4082-8422-12db7c212695",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 49,
    "text": "# equivalent to directions=(\"query_to_doc\",), partition_mode=\"joint\"Symmetric InfoNCE (Günther et al. 2024) — adds the reverse direction so both queries and documents are trained to find their match:\nloss = MultipleNegativesRankingLoss(\nmodel,\ndirections=(\"query_to_doc\", \"doc_to_query\"),\npartition_mode=\"per_direction\",\n)GTE improved contrastive loss (Li et al. 2023) — adds same-type negatives (query <-> query, doc <-> doc) for a stronger training signal, especially useful with pairs-only data:\nloss = MultipleNegativesRankingLoss(\nmodel,\ndirections=(\"query_to_doc\", \"query_to_query\", \"doc_to_query\", \"doc_to_doc\"),\npartition_mode=\"joint\",\n)Hardness-weighted contrastive learning (#3667)",
    "character_count": 691
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "51bc2a2b-e89c-4af3-9bff-2386b98d0e46",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 50,
    "text": "loss = MultipleNegativesRankingLoss(\nmodel,\ndirections=(\"query_to_doc\", \"query_to_query\", \"doc_to_query\", \"doc_to_doc\"),\npartition_mode=\"joint\",\n)Hardness-weighted contrastive learning (#3667)\nAdds optional hardness weighting to MultipleNegativesRankingLoss and CachedMultipleNegativesRankingLoss, inspired by Lan et al. 2025 (LLaVE). This up-weights harder negatives in the softmax by adding hardness_strength * stop_grad(cos_sim) to selected negative logits. The feature is off by default (hardness_mode=None), so existing behavior is unchanged.\nThe hardness_mode parameter controls which negatives receive the penalty:\n- \"in_batch_negatives\": Penalizes in-batch negatives only (positives and hard negatives from other samples). Works with all data formats including pairs-only.\n- \"hard_negatives\": Penalizes explicit hard negatives only (columns beyond the first two). Only active when hard negatives are provided.",
    "character_count": 917
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "b8c76ba8-8e85-488e-a62b-be701dbd661f",
    "technology": "sentence_transformers",
    "source": "release_notes",
    "path": "sentence_transformers/release_notes.txt",
    "chunk_index": 51,
    "text": "- \"hard_negatives\": Penalizes explicit hard negatives only (columns beyond the first two). Only active when hard negatives are provided.\n- \"all_negatives\": Penalizes both in-batch and hard negatives, leaving only the positive unpenalized.\nfrom sentence_transformers.losses import MultipleNegativesRankingLoss\nloss = MultipleNegativesRankingLoss(\nmodel,\nhardness_mode=\"in_batch_negatives\",\nhardness_strength=9.0,\n)New loss: GlobalOrthogonalRegularizationLoss (#3654)\nIntroduces GlobalOrthogonalRegularizationLoss (Zhang et al. 2017), a regularization loss that encourages embeddings to be well-distributed in the embedding space. It penalizes two things: (1) high mean pairwise similarity across unrelated embeddings, and (2) high second moment of similarities (which indicates clustering). This loss is meant to be combined with a primary contrastive loss like MultipleNegativesRankingLoss. By wrapping both losses in a single module, you can share embeddings and only require one forward pass:",
    "character_count": 994
  },
  {
    "chunk_id": "dc72739d-afb3-4c18-9034-f55f034ab843",
